<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_appendix"><a href="#A2" title="In Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref">B </span>Higher torsion of fiber bundles</span></a></li>
<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_bibliography"><a href="#bib" title="In Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">References</span></a></li>
</ol></nav>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Main results</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix a closed, connected, smooth manifold <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote by <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> its 1-jet space, where we recall <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{1}M=T^{*}M\times\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{1}M=T^{*}M\times\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with coordinates <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,p,z)\in J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,p,z)\in J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in T^{*}_{q}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in T^{*}_{q}M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the contact form <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dz-pdq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dz-pdq</annotation></semantics></math> endowing <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> with its standard contact structure. In this article we introduce a family of new K-theoretic Legendrian isotopy invariants for Legendrian submanifolds <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math>, which we call <span id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Legendrian higher torsion</span>. These invariants are defined in terms of generating functions for <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and have several incarnations depending on the class of fiber bundles one chooses to consider as the possible domains of generating functions for <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In general, one has notion of a <span id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">torsion pair</span>, which consists of a pair <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is a fiber bundle and <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\pi_{1}W\to U(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\pi_{1}W\to U(1)</annotation></semantics></math> is a rank 1 unitary local system such that <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}(F;\mathbf{C}^{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}(F;\mathbf{C}^{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math> is a unipotent <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}M</annotation></semantics></math>-module. If <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is closed no more data is needed, but if <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> has boundary or is non-compact one should also fix the behavior near the boundary or at infinity for the class of functions <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:W\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:W\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> we allow ourselves to consider as possible generating functions. For a torsion pair <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> the theory of higher torsion characteristic classes due to the second author and J. Klein produces cohomology classes <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(W,\rho)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(W,\rho)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> which are invariants of <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> up to fibered diffeomorphism of fiber bundles (compatible with the fixed behavior at infinity) and up to the notion of stable equivalence which replaces <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\times\mathbf{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\times\mathbf{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:W\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:W\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\text{s}}(w,x_{1},x_{2},y_{1},y_{2})=f(w)+x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2}-y_{1}^{2}-y_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mtext>s</mtext></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\text{s}}(w,x_{1},x_{2},y_{1},y_{2})=f(w)+x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2}-y_{1}^{2}-y_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Igu02</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Igu05</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">IK93a</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">IK93b</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">K89</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}(F,\mathbf{C}^{\rho})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}(F,\mathbf{C}^{\rho})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math> the class <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{0}(W,\rho)\in H^{0}(M;\mathbf{R})\simeq\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{0}(W,\rho)\in H^{0}(M;\mathbf{R})\simeq\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> recovers the usual Reidemeister torsion of the fiber <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho|_{\pi_{1}F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho|_{\pi_{1}F}</annotation></semantics></math> and for <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> the class <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(W,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(W,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> may be thought of as a parametrized generalization of this Reidemeister torsion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Equipped with the theory of higher torsion classes of fiber bundles, we define the <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th <span id="S1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Legendrian higher torsion</span> of a Legendrian <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> in a fixed collection <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of torsion pairs to be the subset <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})\subset H^{2k}(W,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})\subset H^{2k}(W,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of all degree <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math> higher torsion characteristic classes <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(W,\rho)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(W,\rho)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> of torsion pairs <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> in the class <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> admits a generating function on a stabilization of the fiber bundle <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to M</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})=\coprod_{k}\tau_{k}(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})\subset H^{*}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∐</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})=\coprod_{k}\tau_{k}(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})\subset H^{*}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S1.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">total Legendrian higher torsion</span> of <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> in the fixed collection <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The starting point of the theory of Legendrian higher torsion is the following consequence of the homotopy lifting property for generating functions under Legendrian isotopies (up to stabilization).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The subset <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})\subset H^{*}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})\subset H^{*}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> is a Legendrian isotopy invariant of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For example, in the article <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI</a>]</cite> the first and second authors implicitly gave a concrete application of these ideas in the special case in which <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of oriented circle bundles <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}\to W\to\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}\to W\to\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> over a fixed orientable surface <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, equipped with rank 1 unitary local systems which send the oriented fiber to a fixed <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-root of unity <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm n</annotation></semantics></math> the Euler number of <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>. In that specific case it is computed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI</a>]</cite> that for Legendrians in a certain class (called <span id="S1.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mesh Legendrians</span>), all of which in particular admit generating functions on circle bundles, the degree 2 Legendrian higher torsion <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> of a mesh Legendrian <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\Sigma;\mathbf{R})\simeq\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\Sigma;\mathbf{R})\simeq\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> records the Euler number (up to sign) of the circle bundle that was used to generate it. It follows from this computation that each mesh Legendrian admits a generating function on a circle bundle of Euler number <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm n</annotation></semantics></math> for a unique positive integer <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S1.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">even after stabilization of the circle bundle</span>. In particular it follows that this number is an invariant of the mesh Legendrian.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI</a>]</cite> a variant of Legendrian higher torsion called <span id="S1.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Legendrian Turaev torsion</span> was defined for a general class of Legendrians called <span id="S1.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Euler Legendrians</span>. For mesh Legendrians the Legendrian Turaev torsion allowed one to also recover the sign of this Euler number. The Legendrian Turaev torsion was then used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI</a>]</cite> to distinguish pairs of Legendrians which were formally Legendrian isotopic but not Legendrian isotopic, and in fact such that the isomorphism class of the Legendrian contact homology dga and microlocal sheaf category was unable to distinguish them (at least with traditional coefficients).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Although different flavors of the invariant may be useful for different purposes, our main focus in the present article is a specific version of Legendrian higher torsion which we call <span id="S1.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tube torsion</span>. This version is particularly relevant to the nearby Lagrangian conjecture, the study of which is the primary motivation for this work. Tube torsion is Legendrian higher torsion for the class <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of tube bundles in the sense of Waldahusen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wal82</a>]</cite>. Tube bundles have gained direct relevance to the nearby Lagrangian conjecture after the existence theorem for (twisted) generating functions on tube bundles for nearby Lagrangians proved by Abouzaid, Courte, Guillermou and Kragh in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACGK</a>]</cite>. When working with tube bundles we will omit the class <math id="S1.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> from the notation, and as will be explained, the data of the unitary local system may also be disregarded. Although the precise definition of the invariant will be given in Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem25" title="Definition 3.25. ‣ 3.5. The Legendrian invariant ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.25</span></a> once the notions of tube bundles and generating functions of tube type are properly formulated, we give for now the following provisional definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tube torsion</span> of a Legendrian <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> is the collection <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)\subset H^{*}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)\subset H^{*}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> of higher torsion classes <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(W)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(W)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> of tube bundles <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> on which <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> admits a generating function.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since the class of tubes is <span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stable</span>, i.e. closed under stabilization, we do not have to add <span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">up to stabilization</span> in the above definition.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The main properties of tube torsion are summarized in the following result. We will denote by <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:KO^{0}(M)\to H^{*}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:KO^{0}(M)\to H^{*}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> a suitably normalized version of the Pontryagin character adapted to the definition of higher torsion, see Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Higher torsion of fiber bundles ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. Let <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta(s)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}n^{-s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta(s)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}n^{-s}</annotation></semantics></math> be the standard zeta function. For <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to M</annotation></semantics></math> a real vector bundle we denote <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(E)=\sum_{k}p_{k}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(E)=\sum_{k}p_{k}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(E)\in H^{4k}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(E)\in H^{4k}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> given by</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(E)=\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{k}\zeta(2k+1)ch_{2k}(E\otimes\mathbf{C})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐂</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(E)=\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{k}\zeta(2k+1)ch_{2k}(E\otimes\mathbf{C}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The tube torsion of a Legendrian <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the following:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is invariant under Legendrian isotopy of </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a union of cosets of </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, so we may view </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as a subset of </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{coker}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">coker</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{coker}(p)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for the zero section </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> we have </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)=\text{im}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)=\text{im}(p)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the trivial coset.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> a tube bundle the lack of a unitary local system means we are drawing invariants from <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> rather than <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\mathbf{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\mathbf{C})</annotation></semantics></math> and this forces the odd classes <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k+1}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k+1}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> to be zero. So the only possible nontrivial classes are the even classes <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(W)\in H^{4k}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(W)\in H^{4k}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> and hence the tube torsion of a Legendrian <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> is a disjoint union <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)=\coprod_{k}\tau_{2k}(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∐</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)=\coprod_{k}\tau_{2k}(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(\Lambda)\subset H^{4k}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(\Lambda)\subset H^{4k}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Note that by definition, we have <math id="S1.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> does not admit a generating function on any tube bundle. For example, since the fiberwise Morse theory of a tube bundle in the sense considered in the present article has Euler characteristic <math id="S1.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math> we will certainly have <math id="S1.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math> if the projection <math id="S1.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\to M</annotation></semantics></math> does not have degree <math id="S1.SS1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S1.SS1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math> we say that the tube torsion is <span id="S1.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not defined</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">So for which Legendrians is tube torsion defined? A particularly elusive class of Legendrians is given by the Legendrian lifts of nearby Lagrangians. We recall that for <math id="S1.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> closed, connected, smooth <math id="S1.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional manifolds, a nearby Lagrangian is an exact Lagrangian embedding <math id="S1.SS1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math>. A nearby Lagrangian <math id="S1.SS1.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> admits a generating function on some tube bundle if and only if the stable Gauss map <math id="S1.SS1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math> is nullhomotopic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACGK</a>]</cite>. Therefore the tube torsion of the Legendrian lift <math id="S1.SS1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> of any nearby Lagrangian <math id="S1.SS1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> with stably trivial Gauss map is defined, and we denote it <math id="S1.SS1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(L)\subset H^{*}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(L)\subset H^{*}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACGK</a>]</cite> a procedure for producing a generating function on a tube bundle for a nearby Lagrangian <math id="S1.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> is given once the input of a nullhomotopy of the stable Gauss map <math id="S1.SS1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed, using the <span id="S1.SS1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">doubling trick</span> which Guillermou introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gui12</a>]</cite>. We do not know whether any generating function on a tube bundle for a nearby Lagrangian is given by this procedure. We say that a generating function for <math id="S1.SS1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> which does arise by this procedure is <span id="S1.SS1.p12.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">natural</span> and denote by <math id="S1.SS1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(L)\subset\tau(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(L)\subset\tau(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> the subset consisting of those higher torsion classes in <math id="S1.SS1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> of tube bundles <math id="S1.SS1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> where the generating function for <math id="S1.SS1.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS1.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is natural. We call <math id="S1.SS1.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(L)</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S1.SS1.p12.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">nearby Lagrangian torsion</span> of <math id="S1.SS1.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. It satisfies the following properties:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> be a nearby Lagrangian with trivial stable Gauss map. The nearby Lagrangian torsion of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the following:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(L)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is invariant under Hamiltonian isotopies of </span><math id="S1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(L)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a single coset of </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, so we get a well-defined element </span><math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(L)\in\text{coker}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">coker</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(L)\in\text{coker}(p)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for the zero section we have </span><math id="S1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(M)=\tau(M)=\text{im}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(M)=\tau(M)=\text{im}(p)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.7</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although the tube torsion <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> of the Legendrian lift <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> of a nearby Lagrangian <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> is invariant under Legendrian isotopies of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math>, the nearby Lagrangian torsion <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is not obviously invariant under Legendrian isotopies of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math>. Concretely, if <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1},L_{2}\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1},L_{2}\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> are two nearby Lagrangians with Legendrian isotopic Legendrian lifts, then we do not know whether it must be the case that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(L_{1})=\nu(L_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(L_{1})=\nu(L_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, even though Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.1. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> guarantees that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(L_{1})=\tau(L_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(L_{1})=\tau(L_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 1.8</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACGK</a>]</cite> it was also shown that the stable Gauss map <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math> of a nearby Lagrangian <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> is always trivial on homotopy groups. Since <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> are homotopy equivalent, this implies that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math> is always nullhomotopic when <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> has the homotopy type of a sphere. So for nearby Lagrangian homotopy spheres, generating functions on tube bundles always exist and therefore the tube torsion and nearby Lagrangian torsion of a nearby Lagrangian homotopy sphere are always defined. Both invariants are automatically trivial for degree reasons unless the dimension of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is of the form <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4k</annotation></semantics></math>. For concreteness, say <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=S^{4k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=S^{4k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard sphere (though the discussion is similar if <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is any exotic sphere of dimension divisible by 4). The only possible nontrivial higher torsion class of a tube bundle <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to S^{4k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to S^{4k}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(W)\in H^{4k}(S^{4k};\mathbf{R})\simeq\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(W)\in H^{4k}(S^{4k};\mathbf{R})\simeq\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence for a nearby Lagrangian homotopy sphere <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset T^{*}S^{4k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset T^{*}S^{4k}</annotation></semantics></math> we have a distinguished element</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nu(\Sigma)\in\text{coker}\left(p:KO^{0}(S^{4k})\to H^{4k}(S^{4k};\mathbf{R})\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mtext>coker</mtext><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>K</mi><msup><mi>O</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>;</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(\Sigma)\in\text{coker}\left(p:KO^{0}(S^{4k})\to H^{4k}(S^{4k};\mathbf{R})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, we may view</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(\Sigma)\in\text{coker}\left(\mathbf{Z}\xrightarrow{\lambda_{k}}\mathbf{R}\right)=\mathbf{R}/\lambda_{k}\mathbf{Z}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>coker</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">λ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">k</mi></msub></mover><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(\Sigma)\in\text{coker}\left(\mathbf{Z}\xrightarrow{\lambda_{k}}\mathbf{R}\right)=\mathbf{R}/\lambda_{k}\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}=p(E)\cdot[S^{4k}]\in\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}=p(E)\cdot[S^{4k}]\in\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to S^{4k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to S^{4k}</annotation></semantics></math> the generator of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="KO^{0}(S^{4k})\simeq\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">KO^{0}(S^{4k})\simeq\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. So the nearby Lagrangian torsion of any nearby Lagrangian homotopy sphere <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset T^{*}S^{4k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset T^{*}S^{4k}</annotation></semantics></math> is well-defined and it is just a real number modulo <math id="S1.Thmtheorem8.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">From <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">AD22</a>]</cite> it is known that after a Hamiltonian isotopy we may assume that the front of any nearby Lagrangian homotopy sphere <math id="S1.SS1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset T^{*}S^{4k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset T^{*}S^{4k}</annotation></semantics></math> only has cusp type singularities. When the front of <math id="S1.SS1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> only has cusp type singularities, any generating function <math id="S1.SS1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:W\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:W\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> will have moderate singularities, i.e. Morse or Morse birth/death. See Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1.1. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">When a fibered function of tube type <math id="S1.SS1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> has moderate singularities, from the family of fiberwise Morse chain complexes <math id="S1.SS1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{*}(f_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{*}(f_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in S^{4k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in S^{4k}</annotation></semantics></math>, one may construct a homotopy class of map</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to\text{Wh}^{h}(\mathbf{C},1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>Wh</mtext><mi>h</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to\text{Wh}^{h}(\mathbf{C},1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p14.2" class="ltx_p">and from this one may extract a cohomology class <math id="S1.SS1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(f)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(f)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> as reviewed in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Higher torsion of fiber bundles ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.9</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a nearby Lagrangian homotopy sphere <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset T^{*}S^{4k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset T^{*}S^{4k}</annotation></semantics></math>, after a suitable Hamiltonian isotopy of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> if necessary to ensure the front only has cusp singularities, we have:</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The tube torsion </span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Sigma)\subset\mathbf{R}/\lambda_{k}\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Sigma)\subset\mathbf{R}/\lambda_{k}\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is given by the collection of classes </span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(f)\in\mathbf{R}/\lambda_{k}\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(f)\in\mathbf{R}/\lambda_{k}\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where </span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> ranges over all possible generating functions of tube type </span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:W\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:W\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The nearby Lagrangian torsion </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{2k}(\Sigma)\in\mathbf{R}/\lambda_{k}\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{2k}(\Sigma)\in\mathbf{R}/\lambda_{k}\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is given by the class </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(f)\in\mathbf{R}/\lambda_{k}\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(f)\in\mathbf{R}/\lambda_{k}\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for any given natural generating function of tube type </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:W\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:W\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="156.82" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 468.52 156.82" width="468.52"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,156.82) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(179.69,0) translate(0,63.66)"><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -179.13 0 L -72.49 0"></path><g transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -72.49 0)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.6 0 L -2.16 2.88 L 0 0 L -2.16 -2.88"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -151.57 -27.56 L -151.57 79.08"></path><g transform="matrix(0.0 1.0 -1.0 0.0 -151.57 79.08)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.6 0 L -2.16 2.88 L 0 0 L -2.16 -2.88"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.4pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -148.82 82.68"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -143.93 77.93)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.86em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.49" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.49)" width="11.93"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.F1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M -68.9 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -64.01 -4.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.08em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="14.93"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.F1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M -96.46 77.17"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -115.18 73.71)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.71em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="37.46"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.F1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{F}(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{F}(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -124.02 41.34 C -96.46 41.34 -82.68 49.61 -68.9 60.63"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -124.02 41.34 C -96.46 41.34 -82.68 33.07 -68.9 22.05"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -27.56 0 L 82.68 0"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 -27.56 L 0 82.68"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 2.76 82.68"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 7.64 76.8)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.35em;--ltx-fo-height:0.85em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.75" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 11.75)" width="18.74"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.F1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 82.68 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 87.57 -4.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.08em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="14.93"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.F1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 27.56 41.34 C 55.12 41.34 68.9 49.61 82.68 60.63"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 27.56 41.34 C 55.12 41.34 68.9 33.07 82.68 22.05"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 55.12 68.9 C 55.12 46.85 60.63 23.43 66.14 41.34 C 71.65 70.28 77.17 27.56 77.17 13.78"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 27.56 -4.13 L 27.56 4.13"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 27.56 -4.13"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 24.08 -14.98)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.96)" width="6.96"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.F1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 124.02 0 L 234.25 0"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 151.57 -55.12 L 151.57 55.12"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 162.6 -55.12 L 162.6 55.12"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 170.87 -55.12 L 170.87 55.12"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 179.13 -55.12 L 179.13 55.12"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 187.4 -55.12 L 187.4 55.12"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 195.67 -55.12 L 195.67 55.12"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 203.94 -55.12 L 203.94 55.12"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 212.2 -55.12 L 212.2 55.12"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 220.47 -55.12 L 220.47 55.12"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 179.13 -63.39 L 179.13 63.39"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 179.13 63.39"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 163.46 72.23)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.26em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.49" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.53)" width="31.34"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.F1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{p}^{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{p}^{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 234.25 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 239.14 -4.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.08em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="14.93"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.F1.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 248.03 77.17"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 252.92 72.4)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.26em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.53)" width="31.3"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.F1.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 179.13 0 C 179.13 27.56 220.47 41.34 234.25 44.09"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 234.25 44.09"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 239.14 39.37)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.68em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="9.42"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S1.F1.pic1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 179.13 0 C 179.13 -27.56 220.47 -41.34 234.25 -44.09"></path></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>The front projection <math id="S1.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{F}(\Lambda)\subset M\times\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{F}(\Lambda)\subset M\times\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> of a Legendrian <math id="S1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> near a cuspidal singularities is shown on the left. The realization of <math id="S1.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{F}(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{F}(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> as the Cerf diagram of a family of functions with moderate singularities is shown in middle. The corresponding Lagrangian <math id="S1.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{\ast}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{\ast}M</annotation></semantics></math> has moderate tangencies with respect to the foliation by fibers <math id="S1.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{q}^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{q}^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown on the right.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.SS1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, when the front of a nearby Lagrangian homotopy sphere <math id="S1.SS1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\subset T^{*}S^{4k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\subset T^{*}S^{4k}</annotation></semantics></math> only has cusp type singularities, it is possible to compute tube torsion and nearby Lagrangian torsion of <math id="S1.SS1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> from the parametrized Morse theory of generating functions for <math id="S1.SS1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. A more precise version of this result will be stated and proved in Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Nearby Lagrangian torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we introduce some terminology to distinguish from the case in which nearby Lagrangian torsion isn’t defined.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.10</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We say that a Legendrian <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> has <span id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trivial tube torsion</span> when <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)=\text{im}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)=\text{im}(p)</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that a nearby Lagrangian <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> has <span id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trivial nearby Lagrangian torsion</span> when <math id="S1.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(L)=\text{im}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(L)=\text{im}(p)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.11</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If a nearby Lagrangian <math id="S1.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> has trivial tube torsion, then it automatically has trivial nearby Lagrangian torsion.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">We may formulate the resulting obstruction to Legendrian and Hamiltonian isotopy to the zero section as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.12</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If a Legendrian <math id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> is Legendrian isotopic to the zero section, then it has trivial tube torsion. In particular, if a nearby Lagrangian <math id="S1.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> is Hamiltonian isotopic to the zero section, then it has trivial nearby Lagrangian torsion.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p18.1" class="ltx_p">The famous nearby Lagrangian conjecture states that any nearby Lagrangian <math id="S1.SS1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> is Hamiltonian isotopic to the zero section. The following question is therefore natural.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Question 1.13</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Must a nearby Lagrangian <math id="S1.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> have trivial nearby Lagrangian torsion?</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">An affirmative answer to this question would provide further evidence towards the nearby Lagrangian conjecture, whereas a negative answer would disprove it. Note that according to the nearby Lagrangian conjecture the Legendrian lift <math id="S1.SS1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> of a nearby Lagrangian <math id="S1.SS1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> would in particular be Legendrian isotopic to <math id="S1.SS1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, though even this weaker form of the conjecture is not known. It seems conceivable that there could exist a nearby Lagrangian with trivial nearby Lagrangian torsion but nontrivial tube torsion, which would also disprove the nearby Lagrangian conjecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 1.14</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The invariant <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(L)</annotation></semantics></math> is an invariant of a <span id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">single</span> nearby Lagrangian <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math>. It is a parametrized torsion invariant of a family of acyclic chain complexes associated to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. The space of parameters is <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, and for a single parameter <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math> the relevant chain complex is (at least conjecturally) <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF^{*}(T_{m}M,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF^{*}(T_{m}M,L)</annotation></semantics></math>, see Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5. Floer theoretic perspective ‣ 4. Nearby Lagrangian torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>. However, we take the generating function viewpoint in the present article and use the fiberwise Morse complex <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{*}(f_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{*}(f_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> of a natural generating function <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> instead. The work by Courte and Porcelli <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">CP25</a>]</cite> gives a vanishing result for the parametrized higher torsion of a <span id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">family</span> of nearby Lagrangians <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{z}\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{z}\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math>,<math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in Z</annotation></semantics></math>, where for a single parameter the relevant chain complex is <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF^{*}(M,L_{z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF^{*}(M,L_{z})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> the zero section, though <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">CP25</a>]</cite> also takes the viewpoint of generating functions. The work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">CP25</a>]</cite> is closer to a parametric generalization of the result <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">AK</a>]</cite> of vanishing of Whitehead torsion for a single nearby Lagrangian <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math>, which concerns <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CF^{*}(M,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CF^{*}(M,L)</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, the invariants <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(L)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(L)</annotation></semantics></math> have no <span id="S1.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-parametric</span> analogue; the fiber of a tube bundle is simply connected so its Whitehead group is trivial. It’s possible the two viewpoints may be reconciled by some assembly type map.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p20.1" class="ltx_p">In the broader class of Legendrians which are not necessarily lifts of nearby Lagrangians, we show that the theory of tube torsion is nontrivial in that Legendrians with nontrivial tube torsion abound. In fact, we establish the existence of Legendrians which have nontrivial tube torsion and which are diffeomorphic to the base, so that the conjectural triviality of tube torsion for nearby Lagrangians would in any case not follow from known constraints on the topology of nearby Lagrangians.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p21.1" class="ltx_p">To formulate the precise statement, we will use the viewpoint of Waldhausen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wal82</a>]</cite> where a fibration sequence <math id="S1.SS1.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}\to BG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}\to BG</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed. Here <math id="S1.SS1.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is the space of stable h-cobordisms of a point (which classifies bundles of h-cobordisms of a disk up to stable equivalence), <math id="S1.SS1.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is the stable space of tubes (which classifies tube bundles up to stable equivalence), and <math id="S1.SS1.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BG</annotation></semantics></math> is the classifying space for stable spherical fibrations, so <math id="S1.SS1.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\lim_{n}G_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\lim_{n}G_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS1.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> the space of self homotopy-equivalences of <math id="S1.SS1.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, this means given a bundle <math id="S1.SS1.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\to V\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\to V\to M</annotation></semantics></math> of h-cobordisms of a disk, one may attach <math id="S1.SS1.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> to a trivial tube bundle <math id="S1.SS1.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times T</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.SS1.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard tube, to obtain a tube bundle <math id="S1.SS1.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to W\to M</annotation></semantics></math>. The tube bundle <math id="S1.SS1.p21.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> has the same higher torsion as <math id="S1.SS1.p21.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.15</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\to V\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\to V\to M</annotation></semantics></math> be a bundle of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-cobordisms of a disk and let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> the resulting tube bundle. There exists a Legendrian <math id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> which admits a generating function on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and whose projection to the base <math id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopic to a diffeomorphism. In particular the higher torsion classes <math id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(V)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(V)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> are contained in the tube torsion <math id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)\subset H^{*}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)\subset H^{*}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem15.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p22.1" class="ltx_p">The higher torsion of tube bundles arising from h-cobordism bundles as in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem15" title="Theorem 1.15. ‣ 1.1. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.15</span></a> is in general not contained in the image of the (normalized) Pontryagin character, and hence this result shows that Legendrians with nontrivial tube torsion exist in abundance. Unfortunately, we do not know how to visualize a single explicit example of a Legendrian with nontrivial tube torsion. The simplest possible example would live in <math id="S1.SS1.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{1}S^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{1}S^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Problem 1.16</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Describe explicitly, or draw the front of a Legendrian in <math id="S1.Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{1}S^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{1}S^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> with nontrivial tube torsion.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p23.1" class="ltx_p">Although we do not know whether nearby Lagrangians must always have trivial tube torsion, or even whether they must always have trivial nearby Lagrangian torsion, in the upcoming work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">AIS</a>]</cite> we will prove the triviality of nearby Lagrangian torsion under the additional assumption that the front admits a ruling satisfying certain technical conditions, which we call a <span id="S1.SS1.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">restricted ruling</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p24.1" class="ltx_p">Finally we note that if a nearby Lagrangian <math id="S1.SS1.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> has a stable Gauss map <math id="S1.SS1.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math> which is not nullhomotopic, then it cannot admit any generating function, so that tube torsion is not defined. However, such an <math id="S1.SS1.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> would already be a counterexample to the nearby Lagrangian conjecture, since a Hamiltonian isotopy between <math id="S1.SS1.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and the zero section would in particular yield a nullhomotopy of the stable Gauss map.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Structure of the article</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Legendrian higher torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we define a general class of invariants which we collectively call <span id="S1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Legendrian higher torsion</span>. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we specialize to a specific version tailored to the nearby Lagrangian conjecture, which we call <span id="S1.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tube torsion</span>, and prove the existence of Legendrians which are diffeomorphic to the base and which have nontrivial tube torsion. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Nearby Lagrangian torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we identify a distinguished coset of the tube torsion of a nearby Lagrangian, which we call <span id="S1.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">nearby Lagrangian torsion</span>, and prove that it is a Hamiltonian isotopy invariant. In Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Moderate singularities ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> we give some background on functions with moderate singularities and in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Higher torsion of fiber bundles ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> we give some background on the definition of higher torsion invariants of fiber bundles.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3. </span>Acknowledgements</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are grateful to Sander Kupers for private communication related to the numerical comparison of regulators and higher torsion. The first author is grateful for the support of the NSF under grant DMS-2203455, the Simons Foundation and the K-Theory foundation. The second author is grateful for the support of the Simons Foundation, grant # 686616. The third author is grateful for the support of the Simons Foundation, grant # 019208.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Legendrian higher torsion</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we present a general construction of Legendrian invariants using higher torsion classes, which are certain characteristic classes of fiber bundles of smooth manifolds arising from algebraic K-theory.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Fiber bundles and fibrations at infinity</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed, connected smooth manifold. We consider fiber bundles of smooth manifolds <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> and functions <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:W\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:W\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> on the total space, which we will often think of as families of functions <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{m}:F_{m}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{m}:F_{m}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> on the fiber <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{m}\simeq F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{m}\simeq F</annotation></semantics></math>, parametrized by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>. In the simplest case <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a closed manifold. When <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is not a closed manifold we will have to add conditions to the possible functions <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:F\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:F\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> we consider. We use the terminology of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EG98</a>]</cite> in the following definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A function <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:F\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:F\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fibration at infinity</span> if there exists a finite segment <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-a,a]\subset\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-a,a]\subset\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and a compact subset <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subset f^{-1}[-a,a]\subset F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subset f^{-1}[-a,a]\subset F</annotation></semantics></math> such that the restriction of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to the following three subsets fibers them over their respective images:</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{-1}(-\infty,a]\to(-\infty,a]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(-\infty,a]\to(-\infty,a]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{-1}[a,\infty)\to[a,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}[a,\infty)\to[a,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f^{-1}[-a,a])\setminus K\to[-a,a]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f^{-1}[-a,a])\setminus K\to[-a,a]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As noted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EG98</a>, Section 0.2.1]</cite>, a function <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:F\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:F\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fibration at infinity if and only if <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> admits a complete Riemannian metric with respect to which <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|df\|\geq\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|df\|\geq\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> outside of a compact subset.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following are fibrations at infinity.</p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A non-degenerate quadratic form <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A function <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> of class <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> which is 2-homogeneous, i.e. <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\lambda v)=\lambda^{2}f(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\lambda v)=\lambda^{2}f(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, outside of a compact subset <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\subset\mathbf{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\subset\mathbf{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and such that the function <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:S^{N-1}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:S^{N-1}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(v)=\lim_{\lambda\to+\infty}f(\lambda v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(v)=\lim_{\lambda\to+\infty}f(\lambda v)</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth function which has <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> as a regular value.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A function <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which is a bounded <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-distance from a function as in (2).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The direct sum of two fibrations at infinity on the product domain.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For a first contact with higher Reidemeister torsion it will be sufficient to consider, for <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> a closed manifold, the class of fiber bundles <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> whose fiber is diffeomorphic to <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, together with their stabilizations. By the stabilization of a fiber bundle we will mean the fiberwise product with a trivial <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{4N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{4N}</annotation></semantics></math> bundle, which yields a new fiber bundle</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to W\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to M." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to W\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to M.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">On this bundle (with non-compact fibers) we distinguish the following class of functions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> closed, smooth manifolds and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> a fiber bundle, we denote by <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> the class of all of functions <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:W\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:W\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which are fibrations at infinity of the form</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(w,x,y)=g(w)+\varepsilon(w,x,y)+\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2},\qquad(w,x,y)\in W\times\mathbf{R}^{2N}\times\mathbf{R}^{2N}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(w,x,y)=g(w)+\varepsilon(w,x,y)+\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2},\qquad(w,x,y)\in W\times\mathbf{R}^{2N}\times\mathbf{R}^{2N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:W\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:W\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth function and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth function with compact support. Functions <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:W\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:W\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> in the class <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> will be said to be <span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">admissible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:W\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:W\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is admissible then in particular with respect to any Riemannian metric on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> it remains a bounded <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-distance from the standard fiberwise quadratic form:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(w,x,y)=\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2},\qquad(w,x,y)\in W\times\mathbf{R}^{2N}\times\mathbf{R}^{2N}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(w,x,y)=\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2},\qquad(w,x,y)\in W\times\mathbf{R}^{2N}\times\mathbf{R}^{2N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If instead of starting with a fiber bundle of closed manifolds <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> we started with a fiber bundle whose fiber <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> has boundary (or is non-compact), then some fixed asymptotic behavior near the boundary (or at infinity) for the functions <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:W\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:W\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> as in the above definition should be specified.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_warning">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Warning 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We should really write <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> the fibration but we will abuse notation and use <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(\pi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(\pi)</annotation></semantics></math> throughout.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>The Legendrian invariant</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us first consider the case where the fiber <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is closed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">torsion pair</span> <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a fiber bundle <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> and a unitary local system <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\pi_{1}W\to U(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\pi_{1}W\to U(1)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}(F;\mathbf{C}^{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}(F;\mathbf{C}^{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math> is a unipotent <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}M</annotation></semantics></math>-module.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}(F;\mathbf{C}^{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}(F;\mathbf{C}^{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the homology of the fiber with the twisted complex coefficients given by <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho|_{\pi_{1}F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho|_{\pi_{1}F}</annotation></semantics></math> and by <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unipotent</span> we mean that the <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}M</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}(F;\mathbf{C}^{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}(F;\mathbf{C}^{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math> admits a filtration by <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> submodules so that the action of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> on the successive subquotients is trivial (a basepoint <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in M</annotation></semantics></math> should be chosen but this choice is immaterial).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_warning">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Warning 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We should really write <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> the fibration but we will abuse notation and write <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> throughout.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given a torsion pair <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>, one has its higher Reidemeister torsion, which consists of cohomology classes <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(W,\rho)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(W,\rho)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math>, whose construction is reviewed in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Higher torsion of fiber bundles ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By a <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Legendrian submanifold</span> we will mean throughout a Legendrian embedding <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\hookrightarrow J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\hookrightarrow J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> of a closed smooth manifold <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. compact and without boundary. By an <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">immersed Legendrian</span> we will mean a Legendrian immersion <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\to J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\to J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> of a closed smooth manifold <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, though we will abuse notation and denote both embedded and immersed Legendrians by <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math>. To make the connection between functions on fiber bundles over <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and Legendrians in <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> we need an additional condition on the functions we consider. The condition is generic, and is the standard definition of a <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generating function</span> for a Legendrian in a 1-jet space. Recall that the <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vertical differential</span> of a function <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:W\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:W\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> on the total space of a fiber bundle <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is the restriction <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}f</annotation></semantics></math> of the usual differential <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="df:TW\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">df:TW\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> to the vertical distribution <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker(d\pi)\subset TW" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker(d\pi)\subset TW</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A function <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:W\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:W\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be a <span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generating function</span> if the vertical differential <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}f</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse to zero.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a generating function, then <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> determines a Legendrian immersion from the fiberwise singular locus <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Sigma_{f}=\{d_{v}f=0\}\subset W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{f}=\{d_{v}f=0\}\subset W</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, the condition that <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{v}f\pitchfork 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>⋔</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{v}f\pitchfork 0</annotation></semantics></math> means that the graph <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(df)\subset T^{*}W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(df)\subset T^{*}W</annotation></semantics></math> intersects transversely the coisotropic sumanifold <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset T^{*}W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset T^{*}W</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of covectors which are zero on the fibers <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{m}F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{m}F</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>. The symplectic reduction of the coisotropic submanifold <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally identified with <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> and the image of the submanifold <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\cap\Gamma(df)=\Gamma(df)|_{\Sigma_{f}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\cap\Gamma(df)=\Gamma(df)|_{\Sigma_{f}}</annotation></semantics></math> under this reduction is an immersed exact Lagrangian submanifold of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> whose Legendrian lift (via the values of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>) is an immersed Legendrian submanifold <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">When moreover <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is an embedded Legendrian submanifold of <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> (which is the case generically), one says that the Legendrian submanifold <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generated</span> by <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Now fix a collection <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of torsion pairs <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>. Examples of reasonable collections to consider include but are not limited to:</p>
<ol id="S2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The singleton consisting of a fixed <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and a fixed <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The collection of all <math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> for a fixed <math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> but all possible <math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> for that <math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The collection of all possible torsion pairs <math id="S2.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> with fiber a fixed closed manifold <math id="S2.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> be a Legendrian submanifold. The <span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Legendrian higher torsion</span> of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> in the class <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is the subset <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})\subset H^{*}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})\subset H^{*}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of all higher Reidemeister torsion classes <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(W,\rho)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(W,\rho)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> of torsion pairs <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> admits a generating function <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{S}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{S}(W)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.11</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{0},\Lambda_{1}\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{0},\Lambda_{1}\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> are Legendrian isotopic, then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda_{0},\mathcal{F})=\tau(\Lambda_{1},\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda_{0},\mathcal{F})=\tau(\Lambda_{1},\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">As follows from the homotopy lifting property for generating functions under Legendrian isotopy up to stabilization, if <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> admits a generating function in the class <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(W)</annotation></semantics></math>, then so does <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The desired result then follows immediately. The homotopy lifting property for generating functions under Legendrian isotopies has a long history <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sik86</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">C96</a>]</cite>, but the formulation which is needed at this level of generality may be found in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EG98</a>]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Suitable classes of fiber bundles</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Of course, given a Legendrian <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math>, for most choices of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> we will have <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>, since many Legendrians don’t admit any generating function and even those which do will only admit generating functions on certain fiber bundles. More generally, if one wishes to use generating function theory to study a particular class of Legendrians, then one should make a judicious choice for the class <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of fiber bundles and notion of admissible generating function under consideration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As a first example, if <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is disjoint from a single fiber <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x}M\times\mathbf{R}\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x}M\times\mathbf{R}\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> then it won’t admit a generating function on any fiber bundle <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> with fiber <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> a closed manifold, even after stabilization. In particular, if one wishes to study compact Legendrian submanifolds of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{1}\mathbf{R}^{n}=\mathbf{R}^{2n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{1}\mathbf{R}^{n}=\mathbf{R}^{2n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> one should consider a theory of generating functions with zero fiberwise Morse homology. In the <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> case one could consider as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">JT</a>]</cite> generating functions <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbf{R}\times\mathbf{R}^{k+1}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbf{R}\times\mathbf{R}^{k+1}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> on the trivial <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle over <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐑</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which are linear-quadratic at infinity, i.e. such that for <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w,v,z)\in\mathbf{R}\times\mathbf{R}^{k}\times\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w,v,z)\in\mathbf{R}\times\mathbf{R}^{k}\times\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> there holds outside of a compact subset <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(w,v,z)=Q(v)+L(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(w,v,z)=Q(v)+L(z)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> a nondegenerate quadratic form and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> a nondegenerate linear form.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If instead one is interested in studying Legendrian braids in <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{1}S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{1}S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then one could consider as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx61" title="" class="ltx_ref">T01</a>]</cite> generating functions <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:S^{1}\times\mathbf{R}^{k}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:S^{1}\times\mathbf{R}^{k}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> on the trivial <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle over <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> which are quadratic at infinity, i.e. such that for <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w,v)\in S^{1}\times\mathbf{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w,v)\in S^{1}\times\mathbf{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> there holds outside of a compact subset <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(w,v)=Q(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(w,v)=Q(v)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> a nondegenerate quadratic form.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Other classes of fiber bundles may be useful for other purposes. We explain the simplest example of a nontrivial application of the theory of Legendrian higher torsion known to us, in which the class of fiber bundles under consideration consists of circle bundles over orientable surfaces. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI</a>]</cite> the first two authors investigated a class of Legendrian submanifolds which admit generating functions on such circle bundles. Given an oriented circle bundle over an oriented surface <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}\to W\to\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}\to W\to\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, with Euler number <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, and given a rank 1 unitary local system <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\pi_{1}W\to U(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\pi_{1}W\to U(1)</annotation></semantics></math> such that the oriented fiber <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> gets sent to <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> an <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th root of unity, the higher Reidemeister torsion <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}(W,\rho)\in H^{2}(\Sigma;\mathbf{R})\simeq\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}(W,\rho)\in H^{2}(\Sigma;\mathbf{R})\simeq\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant multiple of the Euler number of the circle bundle up to sign, as was computed by the second author and J. Klein in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">IK93a</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">IK93b</a>]</cite>. Concretely, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}(W,\rho)=n\,\,\text{Im}\left(\mathcal{L}_{2}(u)\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.340em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}(W,\rho)=n\,\,\text{Im}\left(\mathcal{L}_{2}(u)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dilogarithm</p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{2}(z)=\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac{z^{k}}{k^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mfrac><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{2}(z)=\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac{z^{k}}{k^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">That the higher Reidemeister torsion up to sign survives as a stable invariant of the circle bundle and is equal to the Euler class is a nontrivial fact. This fact is a special case of a deep result of Bökstedt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">B82</a>]</cite>, and may also be deduced from the theory of higher Reidemeister torsion, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Igu02</a>]</cite>, concretely Chapter 7 (complexified Bökstedt theorem). In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">IK93a</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">IK93b</a>]</cite> a more direct calculation for this special case is given.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The class of Legendrians <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> which were studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI</a>]</cite> are called <span id="S2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mesh Legendrians</span>. Each mesh Legendrian <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by combintarial data and admits an explicit generating function on a circle bundle of Euler number <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm e_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm e_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> for a non-negative integer <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{\Lambda}\in\mathbf{Z}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{\Lambda}\in\mathbf{Z}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> which is defined in terms of the combinatorial data. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI</a>]</cite> a calculation was carried out to determine the possible 2-parametric fiberwise Morse complex bifurcations that a generating function for the mesh Legendrian <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> could support, on any such circle bundle over <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In particular it was shown that for any <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbf{Z}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbf{Z}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> a mesh Legendrian <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> only admits a generating function on the circle bundle <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}\to W\to\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}\to W\to\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of Euler number <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm n</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=e_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=e_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S2.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">even after stabilization</span> of the circle bundle. Hence for the singleton class <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}=\{(W,\rho)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}=\{(W,\rho)\}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of a circle bundle <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}\to W\to\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}\to W\to\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of Euler number <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:\pi_{1}W\to U(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:\pi_{1}W\to U(1)</annotation></semantics></math> a rank 1 unitary local system which sends the oriented fiber <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math> an <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th root of unity, the Legendrian higher torsion of a mesh Legendrian <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> in the class <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of the singleton</p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})=\{e_{\Lambda}\text{Im}(\mathcal{L}_{2}(u))\}\subset H^{2}(\Sigma;\mathbf{R})\simeq\mathbf{R}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})=\{e_{\Lambda}\text{Im}(\mathcal{L}_{2}(u))\}\subset H^{2}(\Sigma;\mathbf{R})\simeq\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">if <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=e_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=e_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, and is empty otherwise. This proves:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.12</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(implicit in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Legendrian higher torsion of a mesh Legendrian <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> recovers (and uniquely determines) the Euler number (up to sign) of the unique circle bundle that may be used to generate it, even after stabilization of the tube bundle and/or Legendrian isotopy of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In fact in this specific situation the invariant can be refined to a Turaev type torsion which is also sensitive to the sign of the Euler number, and in this refined version the invariant was defined and used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI</a>]</cite> to distinguish Legendrian isotopy classes of mesh Legendrians in <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{1}\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{1}\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> in the same formal Legendrian class. The computations of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI</a>]</cite> can be thought of as a proof of concept that higher torsion invariants may sometimes be calculated for explicit Legendrians.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Our final example of a class <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of fiber bundles which one may want to consider for the definition of a Legendrian higher torsion invariant, and the most important class for the purposes of the present article, is the class of <span id="S2.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tube bundles</span>. Roughly speaking, tube bundles are the most general class of fiber bundles on which one might hope to construct generating functions for <span id="S2.SS3.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">nearby Lagrangians</span>. Hence tube bundles provide the natural class of fiber bundles in which to define a Legendrian higher torsion invariant that may be relevant in the context of the nearby Lagrangian conjecture. We discuss tube bundles and the associated Legendrian higher torsion invariant, which we call <span id="S2.SS3.p8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tube torsion</span>, in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> below.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4. </span>Stable Gauss map</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a Legendrian <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math>, for the Legendrian higher torsion to be defined, i.e. for <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> to be nonempty, in addition to the necessity of making a suitable choice for the class <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of fiber bundles (and unitary local systems) under consideration, it is also necessary that the stable Gauss map <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial, i.e. nullhomotopic Indeed, the triviality of the stable Gauss map is a necessary condition for the existence of <span id="S2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">any</span> generating function, even in a very weak sense. Let us briefly recall the definition of the stable Gauss map.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{Lag}}:J^{1}M=T^{*}M\times\mathbf{R}\to T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>Lag</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{Lag}}:J^{1}M=T^{*}M\times\mathbf{R}\to T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Lagrangian projection and consider the symplectic vector bundle <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=\pi_{\text{Lag}}^{*}T(T^{*}M)|_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mtext>Lag</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=\pi_{\text{Lag}}^{*}T(T^{*}M)|_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is equipped with two polarizations, i.e. Lagrangian sub-bundles, namely <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=T\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=T\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> (since <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{Lag}}|_{\Lambda}:\Lambda\to T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>Lag</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{Lag}}|_{\Lambda}:\Lambda\to T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> is a Lagrangian immersion) and <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\pi_{\text{Lag}}^{*}\nu|_{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mtext>Lag</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\pi_{\text{Lag}}^{*}\nu|_{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> is the Lagrangian distribution on <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> tangent to the fibers of the cotangent bundle projection <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}M\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}M\to M</annotation></semantics></math>. The stable Gauss map measures the stable difference between these two polarizations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Concretely, pick a Lagrangian sub-bundle <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> which is transverse to <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, so that we have a isomorphism of symplectic vector bundles <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\simeq H\oplus H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\simeq H\oplus H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> under which <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> gets identified with <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. There exists some real vector bundle <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\oplus H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\oplus H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a trivial real vector bundle. Then <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}\oplus(H^{\prime})^{*}\oplus E\simeq(H\oplus H^{\prime})\oplus(H\oplus H^{\prime})^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}\oplus(H^{\prime})^{*}\oplus E\simeq(H\oplus H^{\prime})\oplus(H\oplus H^{\prime})^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to a trivial symplectic vector bundle <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\times\mathbf{C}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\times\mathbf{C}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. The Lagrangian sub-bundle <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}\oplus G\subset(H^{\prime}\oplus(H^{\prime})^{*})\oplus E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}\oplus G\subset(H^{\prime}\oplus(H^{\prime})^{*})\oplus E</annotation></semantics></math> may thus be viewed as a Lagrangian sub-bundle of the trivial symplectic vector bundle <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\times\mathbf{C}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\times\mathbf{C}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence gives a map <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\to U_{m}/O_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\to U_{m}/O_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. After letting <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> we get the stable Gauss map <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math>, which is easily verified to be independent of choices up to homotopy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We say that the stable Gauss map <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math> of a Legendrian <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S2.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">trivial</span> when it is nullhomotopic. That the triviality of the stable Gauss map <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math> is a necessary condition for <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> to admit any kind of generating function was shown by Giroux in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">G90</a>]</cite>. In particular, no matter what the class of fiber bundles <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> under consideration is, for Legendrian higher torsion to be defined, i.e. for <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda,\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math> to be nonempty, we must have that the stable Gauss map <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We will say that the front of Legendrian <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> has <span id="S2.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">moderate singularities</span> if the front projection</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\hookrightarrow J^{1}M\to J^{0}M=M\times\mathbf{R}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\hookrightarrow J^{1}M\to J^{0}M=M\times\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">only has cusp singularities. This property is closely related to the triviality of the stable Gauss map. Indeed, for <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> a Legendrian with moderate singularities, consider the co-oriented hypersurface <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,\nu)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is the locus of points whose front have a cusp and <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> is the Maslov co-orientation of <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">A</a>]</cite>. For simplicity let us assume that <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is closed, connected and orientable. The co-oriented cycle <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,\nu)</annotation></semantics></math> determines a class in <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{n-1}(\Lambda;\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{n-1}(\Lambda;\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\dim\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\dim\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, which is Poincaré dual to the Maslov class <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}\in H^{1}(\Lambda,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}\in H^{1}(\Lambda,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>. We have <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if there exists a bounding chain for <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,\nu)</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. a collection of subdomains <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{1},\ldots,\Omega_{k}\subset\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{1},\ldots,\Omega_{k}\subset\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> with disjoint boundaries such that <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\coprod_{j}\partial\Omega_{j}=S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∐</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">∂</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\coprod_{j}\partial\Omega_{j}=S</annotation></semantics></math> and for each component of <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> realized as (part of) the boundary of <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> the Maslov-coorientation <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> on this component agrees with the outwards pointing coorientation determined by <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then have:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.13</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed, connected, smooth manifold and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> a Legendrian embedding. If the front of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> has moderate singularities and if its Maslov class <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}\in H^{1}(\Lambda,\mathbf{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}\in H^{1}(\Lambda,\mathbf{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial, then the stable Gauss map <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial. In fact, any bounding chain for the pair <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,\nu)</annotation></semantics></math> determines a homotopy class of nullhomotopy of the Gauss map <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">When <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> has the homotopy type of a sphere of dimension <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for trivial reasons and the argument is given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">AD22</a>]</cite>. The general case may be proved by a similar line of reasoning, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">B27</a>]</cite> for details. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 2.14</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To be more precise, the statement of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem13" title="Proposition 2.13. ‣ 2.4. Stable Gauss map ‣ 2. Legendrian higher torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13</span></a> should be that any bounding chain determines a homotopy of Lagrangian distributions between the stabilizations of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> in the stabilization of the symplectic vector bundle <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, with notation as in the definition of the stable Gauss map above. So although the stable Gauss map <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math> is only well-defined up to homotopy, we may still speak of a homotopy class of nullhomotopy of the stable Gauss map by defining this to be a homotopy of Lagrangian distributions between the stabilizations of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Note that if <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> is a nearby Lagrangian, then the front of the Legendrian lift of <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> has moderate singularities if and only if the tangencies of <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the foliation of <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> by the cotangent leaves <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}_{m}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}_{m}M</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, has <span id="S2.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">quadratic</span> tangencies. When this is the case we will say that <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> has <span id="S2.SS4.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">moderate tangencies</span>. From Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem13" title="Proposition 2.13. ‣ 2.4. Stable Gauss map ‣ 2. Legendrian higher torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13</span></a> and the fact that nearby Lagrangians always trivial Maslov class <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">A12</a>]</cite> it follows that any nearby Lagrangian with moderate tangencies has stably trivial Gauss map <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math>, and moreover there is a distinguished choice of homotopy class of nullhomotopy given any bounding chain for the cusp locus.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Legendrian tube torsion</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we focus on a specific version of Legendrian higher torsion which we call <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Legendrian tube torsion</span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Generating functions for nearby Lagrangians</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recall that a nearby Lagrangian is an exact Lagrangian submanifold <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> smooth manifolds which are closed, connected and of the same dimension. The nearby Lagrangian conjecture states that any nearby Lagrangian is isotopic through nearby Lagrangians to the zero section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a nearby Lagrangian <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> to admit a generating function on a fiber bundle <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\to W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\to W\to M</annotation></semantics></math>, it is necessary that the fiberwise Morse homology of the generating function have Euler characteristic <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, the projection <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is known to be a homotopy equivalence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">FSS</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Na09</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">A12</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kra13</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gui12</a>]</cite>, and in particular has degree <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The most basic example to keep in mind is the trivial bundle <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}</annotation></semantics></math> with a generating function <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which is equal to the standard fiberwise quadratic form <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> outside of a compact subset, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\in\mathbf{R}^{2N}\times\mathbf{R}^{2N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\in\mathbf{R}^{2N}\times\mathbf{R}^{2N}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will call such generating functions <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">standard quadratic at infinity</span>. Note that the zero-section can be generated by such a function (namely the constant family of standard quadratic forms <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(m,x,y)=\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(m,x,y)=\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>). Therefore, if the nearby Lagrangian conjecture is true, then by the homotopy lifting property for generating functions up to stabilization it would follow that any nearby Lagrangian admits a generating function standard quadratic at infinity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">While existence of such generating functions for nearby Lagrangians is not known, there has been recent progress on existence of generating functions for nearby Lagrangians on the more general class of so-called <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tube bundles</span>. The class of tube bundles is a class of fiber bundles which was introduced by Waldhausen in his manifold approach to the algebraic K-theory of spaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wal82</a>]</cite>. The precise definition of a tube bundle that we will work with in the present article will be given in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Functions of tube type ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> below, but let us note that in particular the fiberwise Morse homology of our notion of <span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generating function of tube type</span> has rank 1 concentrated in a single degree, just like a nondegenerate quadratic form. The foundational result of the article <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACGK</a>]</cite> by Abouzaid, Courte, Guillermou and Kragh is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACGK</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A nearby Lagrangian <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> with trivial stable Gauss map <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math> admits a generating function of tube type.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">So the condition that <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial, which as explained in Section <a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4. Stable Gauss map ‣ 2. Legendrian higher torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> is necessary for the existence of <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">any</span> kind of generating function, is in fact both necessary and sufficient for a nearby Lagrangian <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> to admit a generating function of tube type. It is not known whether every nearby Lagrangian <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> must have stably trivial Gauss map. However, it was shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACGK</a>]</cite> that if <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> is a nearby Lagrangian, then <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\to U/O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\to U/O</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial on homotopy groups. In particular, if <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a homotopy sphere, then any nearby Lagrangian <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> has stably trivial Gauss map and hence there holds:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACGK</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a homotopy sphere, then any nearby Lagrangian <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\subset T^{*}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\subset T^{*}M</annotation></semantics></math> admits a generating function of tube type.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the discussion at the end of Section 3 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACGK</a>]</cite> (see the proof of Theorem 3.26 and preceding lemmas) makes it apparent that tube bundles are in some sense the most general class of fiber bundles on which one may expect a nearby Lagrangian to admit a generating function. The class of tube bundles therefore seems a reasonable class of fiber bundles to study in the context of the nearby Lagrangian conjecture, particularly in the case of nearby Lagrangian homotopy spheres.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Functions of tube type</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Rather than the model for tube bundles and functions of tube type used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACGK</a>]</cite> we will use a convenient model for the space of tubes which was developed in work of the first author joint with Abouzaid, Courte and Kragh in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AACK</a>]</cite>. That Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 ([]). ‣ 3.1. Generating functions for nearby Lagrangians ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> also holds for this model was proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AACK</a>]</cite>. First we will need to introduce a few preliminary notions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">function of quadratic tube type</span> if it satisfies the following:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is homogeneous of degree 2, i.e. <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\lambda v)=\lambda^{2}g(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\lambda v)=\lambda^{2}g(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> has no critical point other than the origin.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopic through functions satisfying (1) and (2) to a non-degenerate quadratic form.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that in general the homogenization of a smooth function <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:S^{N-1}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:S^{N-1}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(v)=\|v\|^{2}h(v/\|v\|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(v)=\|v\|^{2}h(v/\|v\|)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, will only be of class <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A smooth function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">function of tube type</span> if there exists a function of quadratic tube type <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla f-\nabla g\|\leq C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla f-\nabla g\|\leq C</annotation></semantics></math> for some constant <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> exists, then it is uniquely determined by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, indeed <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(v)=\lim_{\lambda\to\infty}\frac{1}{\lambda^{2}}f(\lambda v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(v)=\lim_{\lambda\to\infty}\frac{1}{\lambda^{2}}f(\lambda v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{k,N}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{k,N}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the space of functions of quadratic tube type on <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the non-degenerate quadratic form in condition (3) has index <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{k,N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{k,N}</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of functions of tube type on <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the associated function of quadratic tube type is in <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{k,N}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{k,N}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. We also denote:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{q}_{N}=\coprod_{k=0}^{N}\mathcal{T}^{q}_{k,N},\quad\mathcal{T}^{q}=\coprod_{N=0}^{\infty}\mathcal{T}^{q}_{N},\quad\mathcal{T}_{N}=\coprod_{k=0}^{N}\mathcal{T}_{k,N},\quad\mathcal{T}=\coprod_{N=0}^{\infty}\mathcal{T}_{N}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∐</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∐</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∐</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∐</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{q}_{N}=\coprod_{k=0}^{N}\mathcal{T}^{q}_{k,N},\quad\mathcal{T}^{q}=\coprod_{N=0}^{\infty}\mathcal{T}^{q}_{N},\quad\mathcal{T}_{N}=\coprod_{k=0}^{N}\mathcal{T}_{k,N},\quad\mathcal{T}=\coprod_{N=0}^{\infty}\mathcal{T}_{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Note that if <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}:\mathbf{R}^{N_{i}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}:\mathbf{R}^{N_{i}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a function of tube type for <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}\oplus f_{2}:\mathbf{R}^{N_{1}}\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}\oplus f_{2}:\mathbf{R}^{N_{1}}\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is also of tube type, so we have an operation <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{k_{1},N_{1}}\times\mathcal{T}_{k_{2},N_{2}}\mapsto\mathcal{T}_{k_{1}+k_{2},N_{1}+N_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{k_{1},N_{1}}\times\mathcal{T}_{k_{2},N_{2}}\mapsto\mathcal{T}_{k_{1}+k_{2},N_{1}+N_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> which endows <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> with the structure of a topological monoid. We also have the topological submonoid of functions of quadratic tube type <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{q}\subset\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{q}\subset\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Clearly the inclusion <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{q}\subset\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{q}\subset\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is an equivalence as the linear interpolation <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-t)f+tg" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-t)f+tg</annotation></semantics></math> of a function of tube type <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> with its associated function of quadratic tube type <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> gives a deformation retraction of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We also denote <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{k,N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{k,N}</annotation></semantics></math> for the space of non-degenerate quadratic forms <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> of index <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{N}=\coprod_{k=0}^{N}\mathcal{Q}_{k,N},\qquad\mathcal{Q}=\coprod_{N=0}^{\infty}\mathcal{Q}_{N}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∐</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∐</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{N}=\coprod_{k=0}^{N}\mathcal{Q}_{k,N},\qquad\mathcal{Q}=\coprod_{N=0}^{\infty}\mathcal{Q}_{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{k,N}\subset\mathcal{T}_{k,N}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{k,N}\subset\mathcal{T}_{k,N}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and that <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is a submonoid of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence also of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed, connected, smooth manifold.</p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fibered function of tube type</span> is a smooth function <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for each <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math> the restriction <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is of tube type, i.e. such that <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{m}\in\mathcal{T}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{m}\in\mathcal{T}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fibered function of quadratic tube type</span> is a <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> function <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for each <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math> the restriction <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is quadratic of tube type, i.e. such that <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{m}\in\mathcal{T}_{N}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>q</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{m}\in\mathcal{T}_{N}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fibered function of rigid tube type</span> is a smooth function <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for each <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math> the restriction <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-degenerate quadratic form, i.e. if <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{m}\in\mathcal{Q}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{m}\in\mathcal{Q}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is connected, the index <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of any such fibered function of tube type is well-defined.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To a fibered function of tube type <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> we may associate the fibered function of quadratic tube type <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for each <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math> the restriction <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the function of quadratic tube type associated to the restriction <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Fibered functions of tube type over <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> form a monoid. Fibered functions of quadratic tube type over <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> form submonoid of the monoid of fibered functions of tube type over <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, and fibered quadratic functions of rigid tube type form yet a smaller submonoid.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We will reserve the following terminology for the monoid operation on fibered functions of tube type when one of the two functions is rigid.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be a fibered function of tube type and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be a fibered function of rigid tube type. We call <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\oplus q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{1}}\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\oplus q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{1}}\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">twisted stabilization</span> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">By a <span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stabilization</span> without the adjective <span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">twisted</span> we will mean a stabilization with respect to a constant fibered function of rigid tube type <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x,v)=Q(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x,v)=Q(v)</annotation></semantics></math> for some fixed non-degenerate quadratic form <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, typically we will stabilize by the standard form on <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x,y)=\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x,y)=\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\in\mathbf{R}^{2}\times\mathbf{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\in\mathbf{R}^{2}\times\mathbf{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and will refer to such a stabilization as a <span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">standard stabilization</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be a fibered function of rigid tube type, so that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(m,x)=Q_{m}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(m,x)=Q_{m}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> a family of non-degenerate quadratic forms. Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to M</annotation></semantics></math> be the real vector bundle whose fiber <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math> is the negative eigenspace of the matrix <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\partial^{2}Q_{m}/\partial x_{i}\partial x_{j})_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\partial^{2}Q_{m}/\partial x_{i}\partial x_{j})_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>. We call <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to M</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stable bundle</span> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.10</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, a fibered function of ridid tube type is classified by a map <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to\mathcal{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to\mathcal{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, the notion of stable bundle for a non-degenerate quadratic form determines a map <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{k,N}\to BO(k,N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{k,N}\to BO(k,N)</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence the stable bundle of a fibered function of ridid tube type is classified by the composition <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to\mathcal{Q}\to\mathbf{Z}\times BO" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to\mathcal{Q}\to\mathbf{Z}\times BO</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the map <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{k,N}\to BO(k,N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{k,N}\to BO(k,N)</annotation></semantics></math> is a homotopy equivalence, with homotopy inverse the map <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BO(k,N)\to\mathcal{Q}_{k,N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BO(k,N)\to\mathcal{Q}_{k,N}</annotation></semantics></math> which sends a vector subspace <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subset\mathbf{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subset\mathbf{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> to the quadratic form <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(x)=\|x_{E^{\perp}}\|^{2}-\|x_{E}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(x)=\|x_{E^{\perp}}\|^{2}-\|x_{E}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=x_{E}+x_{E^{\perp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=x_{E}+x_{E^{\perp}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the decomposition of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbf{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbf{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> into its <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\perp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\perp}</annotation></semantics></math> components.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Torsion of fibered functions of tube type</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the second condition in Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 3.3. ‣ 3.2. Functions of tube type ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> is equivalent to asking that <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is transverse to zero outside of the origin, so for a function of quadratic tube type <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> the set <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T(g)=\{g\leq 0\}\cap S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(g)=\{g\leq 0\}\cap S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a codimension-zero submanifold of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> (a priori only <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, but <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> submanifolds have unique smoothings up to contractible choice of isotopy). By the third condition of Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 3.3. ‣ 3.2. Functions of tube type ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> this submanifold is isotopic in <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T(q)=\{q\leq 0\}\cap S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(q)=\{q\leq 0\}\cap S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> a non-degenerate quadratic form. If <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> is the negative eigenspace of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, then this domain is a tubular neighborhood of the equator <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{-}\cap S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{-}\cap S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and in particular is diffeomorphic to <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{k-1}\times D^{N-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{k-1}\times D^{N-k}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> the index of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D^{m}=\{\|x\|\leq 1\}\subset\mathbf{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{m}=\{\|x\|\leq 1\}\subset\mathbf{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> the unit disk.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.11</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rigid tube</span> is the codimension zero submanifold <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(q)\subset S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(q)\subset S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> a non-degenerate quadratic form. The <span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">index</span> of a rigid tube is the index of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.12</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tube</span> is a codimension zero submanifold <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\subset S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\subset S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> which is isotopic in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to a rigid tube. The <span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">index</span> of a tube is the index of any rigid tube it is isotopic to.</p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F2.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="188.98" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 188.98 188.98" width="188.98"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,188.98) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(35.43,0) translate(0,-141.73)"><clipPath id="pgfcp1"><path d="M 59.06 236.22 M 153.54 236.22 C 153.54 288.41 111.24 330.71 59.06 330.71 C 6.87 330.71 -35.43 288.41 -35.43 236.22 C -35.43 184.04 6.87 141.73 59.06 141.73 C 111.24 141.73 153.54 184.04 153.54 236.22 Z M 59.06 236.22"></path></clipPath><g clip-path="url(#pgfcp1)"><g stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,3.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 59.06 236.22 M 153.54 236.22 C 153.54 252.53 111.24 265.75 59.06 265.75 C 6.87 265.75 -35.43 252.53 -35.43 236.22 C -35.43 219.91 6.87 206.69 59.06 206.69 C 111.24 206.69 153.54 219.91 153.54 236.22 Z M 59.06 236.22"></path></g><g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fill-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fg-color:#BFBFBF;" color="#BFBFBF" fill="#BFBFBF" stroke="#BFBFBF"><path d="M 59.06 212.6 M 150.59 212.6 C 150.59 228.91 109.61 242.13 59.06 242.13 C 8.5 242.13 -32.48 228.91 -32.48 212.6 C -32.48 196.29 8.5 183.07 59.06 183.07 C 109.61 183.07 150.59 196.29 150.59 212.6 Z M 59.06 212.6"></path></g><g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fill-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fg-color:#BFBFBF;" color="#BFBFBF" fill="#BFBFBF" stroke="#BFBFBF"><path d="M 59.06 224.41 M 159.45 224.41 C 159.45 240.72 114.5 253.94 59.06 253.94 C 3.61 253.94 -41.34 240.72 -41.34 224.41 C -41.34 208.1 3.61 194.88 59.06 194.88 C 114.5 194.88 159.45 208.1 159.45 224.41 Z M 59.06 224.41"></path></g><g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;" color="#FFFFFF" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF"><path d="M 59.06 236.22 M 153.54 236.22 C 153.54 252.53 111.24 265.75 59.06 265.75 C 6.87 265.75 -35.43 252.53 -35.43 236.22 C -35.43 219.91 6.87 206.69 59.06 206.69 C 111.24 206.69 153.54 219.91 153.54 236.22 Z M 59.06 236.22"></path></g><g></g><g><clipPath id="pgfcp2"><path d="M -29.53 224.41 M -29.53 224.41 L -29.53 283.46 L 141.73 283.46 L 141.73 224.41 Z M 141.73 283.46"></path></clipPath></g><g clip-path="url(#pgfcp2)"><g stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,3.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 59.06 212.6 M 150.59 212.6 C 150.59 228.91 109.61 242.13 59.06 242.13 C 8.5 242.13 -32.48 228.91 -32.48 212.6 C -32.48 196.29 8.5 183.07 59.06 183.07 C 109.61 183.07 150.59 196.29 150.59 212.6 Z M 59.06 212.6"></path></g><g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;" color="#FFFFFF" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF"><path d="M 20.67 177.17 M 20.67 177.17 L 20.67 271.65 L 118.11 271.65 L 118.11 177.17 Z M 118.11 271.65"></path></g><g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fill-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fg-color:#BFBFBF;" color="#BFBFBF" fill="#BFBFBF" stroke="#BFBFBF"><path d="M 20.08 186.02 C 47.24 183.07 53.15 224.41 50.2 248.03 C 50.2 271.65 88.58 271.65 79.72 248.03 C 76.77 236.22 79.72 188.98 119.88 190.57 L 119.88 213.48 C 100.39 212.6 103.35 230.31 109.25 230.31 C 124.02 236.22 124.02 295.28 59.06 295.28 C 20.08 295.28 20.08 252.17 23.62 248.03 C 29.53 236.22 27.76 208.46 20.08 209.35 L 20.08 186.02"></path></g><g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 59.06 236.22 M 153.54 236.22 C 153.54 288.41 111.24 330.71 59.06 330.71 C 6.87 330.71 -35.43 288.41 -35.43 236.22 C -35.43 184.04 6.87 141.73 59.06 141.73 C 111.24 141.73 153.54 184.04 153.54 236.22 Z M 59.06 236.22"></path></g><g></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>A tube <math id="S3.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\subset S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\subset S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
