<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Level sets of standard quadratic forms and corresponding rigid tubes.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.13</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tube bundle</span> is a fiber bundle <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> over a smooth manifold <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<ol id="S3.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is a codimension zero submanifold of <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the map <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is the restriction to <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> of the projection <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times S^{N-1}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times S^{N-1}\to M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">for each <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in M</annotation></semantics></math> the fiber over <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a tube <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x}\subset S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x}\subset S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If for each <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in M</annotation></semantics></math> the fiber of a tube bundle <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is a rigid tube <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x}=T(q_{x})\subset S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x}=T(q_{x})\subset S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{x}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{x}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of nondegenerate quadratic forms, then we call <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> a <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rigid tube bundle.</span> If <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fibered function of quadratic tube type, then we will denote by <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(g)\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(g)\to M</annotation></semantics></math> the associated tube bundle <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T(g)=\{g\leq 0\}\cap S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(g)=\{g\leq 0\}\cap S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fibered function of tube type, we will denote <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(f)=T(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(f)=T(g)</annotation></semantics></math> the tube bundle associated to the underlying fibered function of quadratic tube type <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_warning">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Warning 3.14</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a function of tube type, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(f)</annotation></semantics></math> is not <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{f\leq 0\}\cap S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f\leq 0\}\cap S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{g\leq 0\}\cap S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g\leq 0\}\cap S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)=\|x\|^{2}\lim_{\lambda\to\infty}f(\lambda x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)=\|x\|^{2}\lim_{\lambda\to\infty}f(\lambda x)</annotation></semantics></math>. The same comment applies (fiberwise) for fibered functions of tube type <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and their associated tube bundle <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(f)\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(f)\to M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.15</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We will say that a tube bundle <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> of index <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">orientable</span> if the action of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> on the homology of the fiber <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k-1}(T;\mathbf{Z})\simeq\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k-1}(T;\mathbf{Z})\simeq\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is an orientable tube bundle, then the action of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> on the homology <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}(F)</annotation></semantics></math> of the fiber is trivial and hence in particular unipotent, so we may associate to <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> its higher Reidemeister torsion classes <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(W)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(W)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math>. We emphasize that for orientable tube bundles, no unitary local system is needed. As explained in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Higher torsion of fiber bundles ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, this causes the classes in degrees congruent to 2 modulo 4 to be automatically trivial. So <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k+1}(W)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k+1}(W)=0</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4k+2}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4k+2}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> and we are left with the classes <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(W)\in H^{4k}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(W)\in H^{4k}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that a fibered function of tube type <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> has an associated fibered function of quadratic tube type <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and a corresponding tube bundle <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=T(f)=T(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=T(f)=T(g)</annotation></semantics></math> pver <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.16</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be a fibered function of tube type, with an associated fibered function of quadratic tube type <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and a corresponding tube bundle <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=T(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=T(g)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is orientable, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is called an <span id="S3.Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">orientable</span> fibered function of tube type.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To an oriented fibered function of tube type we may associate to <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> the higher torsion classes <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> of the oriented tube bundle <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=T(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=T(g)</annotation></semantics></math>. These classes are invariant under homotopy of <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> through oriented fibered functions of tube type. For <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> a fibered function of rigid tube type, being orientable amounts to asking that the stable bundle <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to M</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is orientable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let us record the following basic fact.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem17" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem17.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.17</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}=T(f_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}=T(f_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}=T(f_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}=T(f_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> be tube bundles associated to orientable fibered functions of tube type <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopic to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> through fibered functions of tube type, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem17.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> have the same higher torsion classes.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Such a homotopy <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> yields an isotopy of codimension zero submanifolds <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{t}=T(f_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{t}=T(f_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence by isotopy extension a fibered diffeomorphism <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}\simeq W_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}\simeq W_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of tube bundles over <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. Higher torsion classes are invariant under fibered diffeomorphism. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is an orientable fibered function of tube type and <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is an orientable fibered function of rigid tube type, then <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\oplus q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{1}+N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\oplus q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{1}+N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is an orientable fibered function of tube type. Indeed for <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> the index of <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> the index of <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma T(g\oplus q)\simeq\Sigma T(g)\land\Sigma T(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma T(g\oplus q)\simeq\Sigma T(g)\land\Sigma T(q)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{k_{1}+k_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{k_{1}+k_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>-fibrations over <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and hence</p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k_{1}+k_{2}-1}(T(g\oplus q)_{m};\mathbf{R})\simeq H^{k_{1}-1}(T(g)_{m};\mathbf{R})\otimes H^{k_{2}-1}(T(q)_{m};\mathbf{R})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k_{1}+k_{2}-1}(T(g\oplus q)_{m};\mathbf{R})\simeq H^{k_{1}-1}(T(g)_{m};\mathbf{R})\otimes H^{k_{2}-1}(T(q)_{m};\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Hence if both the rank 1 real vector bundles <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k_{1}-1}(T(g)_{m};\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k_{1}-1}(T(g)_{m};\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k_{2}-1}(T(q)_{m};\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k_{2}-1}(T(q)_{m};\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> are trivial, then so is <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k_{1}+k_{2}-1}(T(g\oplus q)_{m};\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k_{1}+k_{2}-1}(T(g\oplus q)_{m};\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math>. At this point it is not yet clear how the higher torsion of <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(g)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(g\oplus q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(g\oplus q)</annotation></semantics></math> should be related. For this purpose we introduce a computational tool.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">If an orientable fibered function of tube type <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> has <span id="S3.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">moderate singularities</span> (i.e. Morse or Morse birth/death, see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Moderate singularities ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>), then it is possible to compute the resulting higher torsion classes from the parametrized Morse theory of <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> directly, and this will be useful to us in what follows. We use the same notation as in the statement of the <span id="S3.SS3.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">framing principle</span> given in Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem B.3. ‣ B.5. The framing principle ‣ Appendix B Higher torsion of fiber bundles ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>. We recall briefly the relevant notions:</p>
<ol id="S3.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> is a cohomology class in <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from the family <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{*}(f_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{*}(f_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, of Morse chain complexes for the fiberwise restrictions <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is a stable real vector bundle over the fiberwise singular set <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{f}\subset M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{f}\subset M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> formed by the negative eigenspaces of the fiberwise Hessian of <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}^{\Sigma f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}^{\Sigma f}</annotation></semantics></math> is the push-down operator in cohomology <math id="S3.I4.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\Sigma_{f})\to H^{*}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\Sigma_{f})\to H^{*}(M)</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="S3.I4.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi|_{\Sigma_{f}}:\Sigma_{f}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi|_{\Sigma_{f}}:\Sigma_{f}\to M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S3.I4.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to X</annotation></semantics></math> a real vector bundle over a manifold <math id="S3.I4.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I4.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(E)\in H^{4k}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(E)\in H^{4k}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> is the normalized Pontryagin character:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(E)=\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{k}\zeta(2k+1)ch_{2k}(E\otimes\mathbf{C})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐂</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(E)=\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{k}\zeta(2k+1)ch_{2k}(E\otimes\mathbf{C}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem18" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.18</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem18.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To simplify issues with signs, from now on we will restrict to the case of even index and ambient dimension a multiple of four. Standard stabilization is by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> with the quadratic form <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem18.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\in\mathbf{R}^{2}\times\mathbf{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\in\mathbf{R}^{2}\times\mathbf{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which will preserve higher torsion, including its sign.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem19" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.19</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be an orientable fibered function of tube type with moderate singularities, with associated fibered function of quadratic tube type <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and tube bundle <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=T(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=T(g)</annotation></semantics></math>. The tube torsion <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(W)=\sum_{k}\tau_{k}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(W)=\sum_{k}\tau_{k}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> may be computed from <math id="S3.Thmtheorem19.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> by the following formula:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(W)=-\left(\tau_{2k}(f)+\pi_{*}^{\Sigma f}p_{k}(\gamma_{f})\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(W)=-\left(\tau_{2k}(f)+\pi_{*}^{\Sigma f}p_{k}(\gamma_{f})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">The discussion of relative higher torsion in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS6" title="B.6. Fibers with boundary ‣ Appendix B Higher torsion of fiber bundles ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.6</span></a> applies to the trivial disk bundle <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{4N}\to X\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{4N}\to X\to M</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=M\times D^{4N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=M\times D^{4N}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the sub-bundle of the sphere bundle <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{4N-1}\to\partial W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{4N-1}\to\partial W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=T(g)\subset M\times S^{4N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=T(g)\subset M\times S^{4N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial we have <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0=\tau_{k}(X)=\tau_{k}(X,W)+\tau_{k}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0=\tau_{k}(X)=\tau_{k}(X,W)+\tau_{k}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(W)=-\tau_{k}(X,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(W)=-\tau_{k}(X,W)</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof is them completed by applying the framing principle Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem B.3. ‣ B.5. The framing principle ‣ Appendix B Higher torsion of fiber bundles ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a> to the bundle pair <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,W)</annotation></semantics></math>, since we may compute the relative higher torsion <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(X,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(X,W)</annotation></semantics></math> using the function with moderate singularities <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem20" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem20.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.20</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be an orientable fibered function of tube type and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be a constant fibered function of rigid tube type, i.e. <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(m,v)=Q(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(m,v)=Q(v)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q:\mathbf{R}^{4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q:\mathbf{R}^{4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> a fixed non-degenerate quadratic form of even index. The higher torsions of the tube bundles <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(f)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem20.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(g\oplus q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(g\oplus q)</annotation></semantics></math> are equal:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(T(f\oplus q))=\tau_{k}(T(f))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(T(f\oplus q))=\tau_{k}(T(f))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the framed function Theorem <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem A.1. ‣ A.3. Framed functions ‣ Appendix A Moderate singularities ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> there exists a function <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which is equal to <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> outside of a compact set and which has moderate singularities. By Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem19" title="Proposition 3.19. ‣ 3.3. Torsion of fibered functions of tube type ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.19</span></a> we may compute the torsion of <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(f)=T(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(f)=T(h)</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. Both <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(h)</annotation></semantics></math> and the stable bundle term <math id="S3.SS3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> are unchanged after stabilization, see Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Higher torsion of fiber bundles ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. The conclusion follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, the classes <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=T(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=T(f)</annotation></semantics></math> are invariant under stabilization of <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> by the standard quadratic form <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS3.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{2}\times\mathbf{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{2}\times\mathbf{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which has index 2. More generally, we have:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem21" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem21.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.21</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be an orientable fibered function of tube type of even index and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be an orientable fibered function of rigid tube type of even index. The higher torsions of the tube bundles <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(f)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(f\oplus q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(f\oplus q)</annotation></semantics></math>are related by the formula</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(T(f\oplus q))=\tau_{k}(T(f))-p_{k}(E)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(T(f\oplus q))=\tau_{k}(T(f))-p_{k}(E)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is the stable bundle of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem21.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the framed function Theorem <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem A.1. ‣ A.3. Framed functions ‣ Appendix A Moderate singularities ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> there exists a function <math id="S3.SS3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> which is equal to <math id="S3.SS3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> outside of a compact set and which has moderate singularities Note that <math id="S3.SS3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{h\oplus q}=\gamma_{h}\oplus(\pi|_{\Sigma h})^{*}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{h\oplus q}=\gamma_{h}\oplus(\pi|_{\Sigma h})^{*}E</annotation></semantics></math> as stable real vector bundles over the fiberwise critical set <math id="S3.SS3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{h}\subset M\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{h}\subset M\times\mathbf{R}^{N_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}^{\Sigma h}ch_{2k}(\gamma_{h\oplus q}\otimes\mathbf{C})=\pi_{*}^{\Sigma h}ch_{2k}(\gamma_{h}\otimes\mathbf{C})+\pi_{*}^{\Sigma h}\pi^{*}ch_{2k}(E\otimes\mathbf{C})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐂</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐂</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐂</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}^{\Sigma h}ch_{2k}(\gamma_{h\oplus q}\otimes\mathbf{C})=\pi_{*}^{\Sigma h}ch_{2k}(\gamma_{h}\otimes\mathbf{C})+\pi_{*}^{\Sigma h}\pi^{*}ch_{2k}(E\otimes\mathbf{C}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">The proof is completed by applying Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem19" title="Proposition 3.19. ‣ 3.3. Torsion of fibered functions of tube type ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.19</span></a> to <math id="S3.SS3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\oplus q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\oplus q</annotation></semantics></math> while noting that <math id="S3.SS3.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{*}^{\Sigma h}\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{*}^{\Sigma h}\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is multiplication by the Euler characteristic of the fibrewise Morse complex which is <math id="S3.SS3.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math> (since the index is even).∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Generating functions of tube type</h3>

<div id="S3.Thmtheorem22" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem22.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.22</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A generating function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:W\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:W\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> for a Legendrian <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generating function of tube type</span> if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem22.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fibered function of tube type.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem23" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem23.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.23</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose a closed Legendrian <math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by a fibered function of tube type <math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{t}\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{t}\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> be a Legendrian isotopy. Then there exists a homotopy of fibered functions of tube type <math id="S3.Thmtheorem23.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\times\mathbf{R}^{4k}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}:M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}\times\mathbf{R}^{4k}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I5" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> generates </span><math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}(m,x,u,v)=f(m,x)+Q(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}(m,x,u,v)=f(m,x)+Q(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for the standard quadratic form </span><math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(u,v)=\|u\|^{2}-\|v\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(u,v)=\|u\|^{2}-\|v\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where </span><math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,x)\in M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,x)\in M\times\mathbf{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)\in\mathbf{R}^{2k}\times\mathbf{R}^{2k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)\in\mathbf{R}^{2k}\times\mathbf{R}^{2k}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I5.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}(m,x,u,v)=f(m,x)+\varepsilon_{t}(m,x,u,v)+Q(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}(m,x,u,v)=f(m,x)+\varepsilon_{t}(m,x,u,v)+Q(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S3.I5.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> a compactly supported function.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows immediately from the general form of the homotopy lifting property for generating functions as is formulated in Theorem 4.1.1. of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">EG98</a>]</cite>. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem24" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem24.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.24</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem24.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by a fibered function of tube type <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, then any twisted stabilization <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\oplus q:\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}+4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\oplus q:\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}+4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> by a fibered function of rigid tube type <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> will also generate <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the monoid of fibered functions of rigid tube type over <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the space of generating functions of tube type for the fixed Legendrian <math id="S3.Thmtheorem24.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5. </span>The Legendrian invariant</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed Legendrian. Denote by <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> the collection of all tube bundles <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> of the form <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=T(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=T(f)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> an orientable fibered function of tube type of even index which is also a generating function for <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem25" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem25.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.25</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the Legendrian tube torsion of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, or <span id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tube torsion</span> for short, to be the subset <math id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)\subset H^{*}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)\subset H^{*}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of all <span id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">negatives</span> of higher torsion classes <math id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\tau_{k}(W)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\tau_{k}(W)\in H^{2k}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> an orientable tube bundle <math id="S3.Thmtheorem25.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathcal{T}(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathcal{T}(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem26" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem26.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.26</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem26.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The reason for defining <math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> to consist of the negatives <math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\tau_{k}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\tau_{k}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem26.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> is Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem19" title="Proposition 3.19. ‣ 3.3. Torsion of fibered functions of tube type ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.19</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1.1. Main results ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove one property at a time:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I6" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">That the tube torsion <math id="S3.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.I6.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is invariant under Legendrian isotopies of <math id="S3.I6.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> follows from Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem23" title="Lemma 3.23. ‣ 3.4. Generating functions of tube type ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.23</span></a> and Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem20" title="Proposition 3.20. ‣ 3.3. Torsion of fibered functions of tube type ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.20</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">That <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is always a (possibly empty) union of cosets of <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> follows from Remark <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem24" title="Remark 3.24. ‣ 3.4. Generating functions of tube type ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.24</span></a> and Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem21" title="Proposition 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Torsion of fibered functions of tube type ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.21</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">That the tube torsion of the zero section <math id="S3.I6.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of <math id="S3.I6.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> follows from Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem19" title="Proposition 3.19. ‣ 3.3. Torsion of fibered functions of tube type ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.19</span></a>, since the parametrized Morse theory of a generating function <math id="S3.I6.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> for the zero section is constant, so <math id="S3.I6.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(f)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(f)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence the only term to contribute is <math id="S3.I6.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{k}(\gamma_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{k}(\gamma_{f})</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S3.I6.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> may be any stable real vector bundle over <math id="S3.I6.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{f}\simeq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>≃</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{f}\simeq M</annotation></semantics></math> as any family of non-degenerate quadratic forms <math id="S3.I6.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I6.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, generates the zero-section.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem27" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem27.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.27</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that a Legendrian <math id="S3.Thmtheorem27.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> admits a generating function of tube type. Then it admits an orientable generating function of tube type.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose that a generating function of tube type <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is not orientable. Let <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to M</annotation></semantics></math> denote the rank 1 vector bundle formed by the real degree <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> cohomology of the fiber <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of the tube bundle <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to M</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the index, i.e. <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{m}=H^{k-1}(T(g)_{m};\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{m}=H^{k-1}(T(g)_{m};\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> the underlying fibered function of quadratic tube type. Let <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{4d}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{4d}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math> be a family of non-degenerate quadratic forms of even index <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2r</annotation></semantics></math> whose stable bundle is (stably) isomorphic to <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. Put <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(m,x)=Q_{m}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(m,x)=Q_{m}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, a fibered function of rigid tube type. Then <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\oplus q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\oplus q</annotation></semantics></math> also generates <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, and is orientable because for <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math> we have isomorphisms</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k+r-1}(T(g\oplus q)_{m};\mathbf{R})\simeq H^{k-1}(T(g)_{m};\mathbf{R})\otimes H^{r-1}(T(q)_{m};\mathbf{R})\simeq E_{m}\otimes E_{m}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k+r-1}(T(g\oplus q)_{m};\mathbf{R})\simeq H^{k-1}(T(g)_{m};\mathbf{R})\otimes H^{r-1}(T(q)_{m};\mathbf{R})\simeq E_{m}\otimes E_{m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">and <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\otimes E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\otimes E</annotation></semantics></math> is orientable. Hence <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\oplus q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\oplus q</annotation></semantics></math> is an orientable generating function of tube type for <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the next result note first that if the front of Legendrian <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> only has cusp singularities, then any generating function of tube type <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> automatically has moderate singularities. Recollections on functions with moderate singularities are given in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Moderate singularities ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem28" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem28.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.28</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem28.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> be a Legendrian whose front <math id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\hookrightarrow J^{1}M\to J^{0}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\hookrightarrow J^{1}M\to J^{0}M</annotation></semantics></math> only has cusp singularities and whose projection to the base <math id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is a homotopy equivalence. If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> admits a generating function of tube type <math id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, then for a suitable fibered function of rigid tube type <math id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> the direct sum <math id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\oplus q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}+4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\oplus q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}+4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a frameable generating function of tube type , i.e. such that the stable bundle <math id="S3.Thmtheorem28.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{f\oplus q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{f\oplus q}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have a canonical diffeomorphism <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{f}\to\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{f}\to\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> under which the projection <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:\Lambda\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:\Lambda\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is the projection <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\Sigma f}:\Sigma_{f}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\Sigma f}:\Sigma_{f}\to M</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\Sigma f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\Sigma f}</annotation></semantics></math> is a homotopy equivalence. Hence there exists a real stable bundle <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to M</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi^{\Sigma f})^{*}E\simeq\gamma_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi^{\Sigma f})^{*}E\simeq\gamma_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now let <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> be a stable even dimensional real vector bundle such that <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\oplus E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\oplus E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial. Let <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> be a fibered quadratic generating function of rigid tube type whose stable bundle is isomorphic to <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{f\oplus q}\simeq\gamma_{f}\oplus(\pi^{\Sigma f})^{*}E^{\prime}\simeq(\pi^{\Sigma f})^{*}(E\oplus E^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{f\oplus q}\simeq\gamma_{f}\oplus(\pi^{\Sigma f})^{*}E^{\prime}\simeq(\pi^{\Sigma f})^{*}(E\oplus E^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Using Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem28" title="Proposition 3.28. ‣ 3.5. The Legendrian invariant ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.28</span></a> we obtain in particular a result which relates <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> to the parametrized theory of a generating function of tube type with moderate singularties <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Concretely, in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Higher torsion of fiber bundles ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> it is explained how to such an <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> one obtains cohomology classes <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(f)\in H^{4k}(M;\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(f)\in H^{4k}(M;\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math> out of the parametrized Morse complex <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{*}(f_{m};\mathbf{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{*}(f_{m};\mathbf{R})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem29" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem29.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.29</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem29.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math> be a Legendrian whose front projection <math id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\hookrightarrow J^{1}M\to J^{0}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\hookrightarrow J^{1}M\to J^{0}M</annotation></semantics></math> only has cusp singularities and whose projection to the base <math id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is a homotopy equivalence. The higher torsion of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is the subset <math id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(\Lambda)\subset\text{coker}(p_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">coker</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(\Lambda)\subset\text{coker}(p_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of the cosets of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{im}(p_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{im}(p_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> with representatives given by the cohomology classes</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(f)\in H^{4k}(M;\mathbf{R})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(f)\in H^{4k}(M;\mathbf{R}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> ranges through the collection of orientable generating functions of tube type (of even index) for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem29.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">First we show that for any such <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\times\mathbf{R}^{4N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(f)\in\tau_{2k}(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(f)\in\tau_{2k}(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, we certainly have that <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=T(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=T(f)</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\tau_{2k}(W)\in\tau_{2k}(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\tau_{2k}(W)\in\tau_{2k}(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>. By Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem19" title="Proposition 3.19. ‣ 3.3. Torsion of fibered functions of tube type ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.19</span></a> we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\tau_{2k}(W)=\tau_{2k}(f)+(\pi^{\Sigma f})_{*}p_{k}(\gamma_{f})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\tau_{2k}(W)=\tau_{2k}(f)+(\pi^{\Sigma f})_{*}p_{k}(\gamma_{f}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\Sigma f}:\Sigma_{f}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\Sigma f}:\Sigma_{f}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is a homotopy equivalence, we have <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{f}=(\pi^{\Sigma f})^{*}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{f}=(\pi^{\Sigma f})^{*}E</annotation></semantics></math> for some real vector bundle <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to M</annotation></semantics></math> and hence</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi^{\Sigma f})_{*}p_{k}(\gamma_{f})=(\pi^{\Sigma f})_{*}(\pi^{\Sigma f})^{*}p_{k}(E)=p_{k}(E)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi^{\Sigma f})_{*}p_{k}(\gamma_{f})=(\pi^{\Sigma f})_{*}(\pi^{\Sigma f})^{*}p_{k}(E)=p_{k}(E),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">where the last equality follows from the fact that <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi^{\Sigma f})_{*}(\pi^{\Sigma f})^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi^{\Sigma f})_{*}(\pi^{\Sigma f})^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is multiplication by the Euler characteristic of the fiber, i.e. <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> is in the same coset of <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{im}(p_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{im}(p_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\tau_{k}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\tau_{k}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(f)\in\tau_{2k}(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(f)\in\tau_{2k}(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> as desired.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, let <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\in\mathcal{T}(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\in\mathcal{T}(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>. Given any generating function of tube type <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{1}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=T(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=T(f)</annotation></semantics></math>, Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem21" title="Proposition 3.21. ‣ 3.3. Torsion of fibered functions of tube type ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.21</span></a> implies that for any fibered function of rigid tube type <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:M\times\mathbf{R}^{4N_{2}}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> the torsion class <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the torsion class <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(T(f\oplus q))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(T(f\oplus q))</annotation></semantics></math> up to an element of <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{im}(p_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{im}(p_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>. If we take <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\oplus q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\oplus q</annotation></semantics></math> is frameable as in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem28" title="Proposition 3.28. ‣ 3.5. The Legendrian invariant ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.28</span></a> then <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(T(f\oplus q))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(T(f\oplus q))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(f\oplus q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(f\oplus q)</annotation></semantics></math> are equal (the extra term in Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem B.3. ‣ B.5. The framing principle ‣ Appendix B Higher torsion of fiber bundles ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a> from the stable bundle vanishes since <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{f\oplus q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{f\oplus q}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial). Finally <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(f)=\tau_{2k}(f\oplus q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(f)=\tau_{2k}(f\oplus q)</annotation></semantics></math> because the fiberwise Morse theory is unchanged by stabilization (and the parity of indices is unchanged). Hence <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2k}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2k}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> are in the same coset of <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{im}(p_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>im</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{im}(p_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> and we are done. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6. </span>The space of tubes</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We briefly compare the model for the space of tubes considered in this article with that of Waldhausen <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wal82</a>]</cite> in order to translate some relevant results from the literature. Recall that <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the space of all functions <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> of tube type, see Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Definition 3.4. ‣ 3.2. Functions of tube type ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. The space <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math> deformation retracts onto the subspace <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> of functions <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> of quadratic tube type, see Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 3.3. ‣ 3.2. Functions of tube type ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Any tube <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\subset S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\subset S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> has two non-negative integers associated to it, namely the ambient dimension <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, so that <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\subset S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\subset S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a codimension zero submanifold, and its index <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the integer <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\subset S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\subset S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is isotopic to <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(q)\subset S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(q)\subset S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> a non-degenerate quadratic form of index <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S3.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">standard tube</span> of type <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N,k)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T_{N,k}=T(q_{k,N})=\{q_{k,N}\leq 0\}\cap S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{N,k}=T(q_{k,N})=\{q_{k,N}\leq 0\}\cap S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{k,N}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{k,N}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> the standard index <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> quadratic form <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\in\mathbf{R}^{N-k}\times\mathbf{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\in\mathbf{R}^{N-k}\times\mathbf{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition a <span id="S3.SS6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tube</span> of type <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N,k)</annotation></semantics></math> is a codimension zero submanifold <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\subset S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\subset S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> which is isotopic to the standard tube of type <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N,k)</annotation></semantics></math>. One may consider the space <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math> of all tubes of type <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N,k)</annotation></semantics></math>, for example using a simplicial space model, or otherwise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Any reasonable model for <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math> would come equipped with a map <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{q}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{q}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\mapsto T(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\mapsto T(g)</annotation></semantics></math> whose fiber may be identified with the space of all functions <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:S^{N-1}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:S^{N-1}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T=\{h\leq 0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\{h\leq 0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial T=\{h=0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial T=\{h=0\}</annotation></semantics></math> a regular level set of <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. For fixed <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, the space of such <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is convex, hence contractible. Such a function <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> determines a unique function of quadratic tube type <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(v)=\|v\|^{2}h(v/\|v\|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(v)=\|v\|^{2}h(v/\|v\|)</annotation></semantics></math>. For any reasonable model the map <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{q}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{q}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Serre fibration and hence one has a weak equivalence <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{q}\simeq\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><mo>≃</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{q}\simeq\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence also a weak equivalence <math id="S3.SS6.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{N,k}\simeq\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≃</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{N,k}\simeq\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Waldhausen considered in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wal82</a>]</cite> the following similar though slightly different notion of tubes in terms of partitions. For <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> a <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional linear subspace of <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{N-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{N-2}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the codimension zero submanifold with boundary <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{E}\subset\mathbf{R}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{E}\subset\mathbf{R}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by attaching to the half-space <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{x_{N-1}\leq 0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{N-1}\leq 0\}</annotation></semantics></math> inside <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> a standard <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional index <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> handle along the unit sphere of <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E\subset\{x_{N-1}=0\}=\mathbf{R}^{N-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subset\{x_{N-1}=0\}=\mathbf{R}^{N-2}</annotation></semantics></math>. More concretely, <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is the union in <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> of the half-space <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{x_{N-1}\leq 0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{N-1}\leq 0\}</annotation></semantics></math> and an <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-neighborhood of the <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional unit sphere of the <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspace of <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> spanned by <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\oplus 0\subset\mathbf{R}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\oplus 0\subset\mathbf{R}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial/\partial x_{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial/\partial x_{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, suitably smoothed so that <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth codimension zero submanifold with boundary. Waldhausen called such a codimension zero submanifold <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{E}\subset\mathbf{R}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{E}\subset\mathbf{R}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> a <span id="S3.SS6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rigid tube</span>. Since the thickening and smoothing are homotopically canonical, for fixed <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> the space of rigid tubes <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> as above has the homotopy type of <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BO(k,N-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BO(k,N-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the Grassmannian of real <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional linear subspaces of <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Waldhausen defined a <span id="S3.SS6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tube</span> to be any codimension zero submanifold <math id="S3.SS6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\subset\mathbf{R}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\subset\mathbf{R}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> which is the image of a rigid tube under a smooth isotopy of <math id="S3.SS6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, fixed outside of a compact set. We will denote the space of tubes in the sense of Waldhausen by <math id="S3.SS6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}_{N,k}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}_{N,k}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> stands for <span id="S3.SS6.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">partition</span>. We may also form a suitable space of functions of tube type <math id="S3.SS6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbf{R}^{N-1}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbf{R}^{N-1}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> in the sense of Waldhausen, (so that in particular <math id="S3.SS6.p5.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{f\leq 0\}\subset\mathbf{R}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f\leq 0\}\subset\mathbf{R}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a tube in the sense of Waldhausen) and denote it by <math id="S3.SS6.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Any reasonable model would again come equipped with an equivalence <math id="S3.SS6.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{p}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}_{N,k}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{p}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}_{N,k}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="S3.SS6.p5.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f\mapsto\{f\leq 0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\mapsto\{f\leq 0\}</annotation></semantics></math>. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACGK</a>]</cite> for a precise definition of a space of functions of tube type very close to the space <math id="S3.SS6.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> described above.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The difference between the model <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math> for the space of tubes considered in this article and the Waldhausen model <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}_{N,k}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}_{N,k}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of partitions, at the <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N,k)</annotation></semantics></math> level, amounts to requiring the tubes in <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math> to be standard in a fixed <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional closed ball <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subset S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subset S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, say <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B=\{x_{N}\geq 0\}\cap S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\{x_{N}\geq 0\}\cap S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. More concretely, with respect to the fixed diffeomorphism <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi:D^{N-1}\to B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi:D^{N-1}\to B</annotation></semantics></math> given by</p>
<table id="S3.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(x_{1},\ldots,x_{N-1})=\left(x_{1},\ldots,x_{N-1},\sqrt{1-\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}x_{i}^{2}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(x_{1},\ldots,x_{N-1})=\left(x_{1},\ldots,x_{N-1},\sqrt{1-\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}x_{i}^{2}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p6.2" class="ltx_p">the precise condition is that a tube <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\subset S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\subset S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> must intersect <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in the half-space <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{x_{N-1}\leq 0\}\cap D^{N-1}\subset D^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{N-1}\leq 0\}\cap D^{N-1}\subset D^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to the identification <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>. One may then forget about <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\cap B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\cap B</annotation></semantics></math> and look at the intersection of <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with the other hemisphere <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{x_{N}\leq 0\}\cap S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{N}\leq 0\}\cap S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which becomes a partition tube in the sense of Waldhausen upon identifying the open hemisphere <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{x_{N}&lt;0\}\cap S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{N}&lt;0\}\cap S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> by a fixed radial diffeomorphism <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{N-1}\xrightarrow{\simeq}\mathbf{R}^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≃</mo></mover><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{N-1}\xrightarrow{\simeq}\mathbf{R}^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the image of <math id="S3.SS6.p6.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T\cap\{x_{N}&lt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\cap\{x_{N}&lt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math> is standard at infinity as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wal82</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">If we define <math id="S3.SS6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}_{N,k}^{p}\subset\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}_{N,k}^{p}\subset\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the subspace of <math id="S3.SS6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of tubes which are standard on <math id="S3.SS6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> as above we get an map given by inclusion <math id="S3.SS6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}_{N,k}^{p}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}_{N,k}^{p}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math>, see Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.6. The space of tubes ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F4.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="167.21" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 394.26 167.21" width="394.26"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,167.21) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(158.04,0) translate(0,9.45)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 28.35 75.59"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 23.16 72.13)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.75em;--ltx-fo-height:0.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.45" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.69)" width="10.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F4.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\longrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\longrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#D9D9D9;--ltx-fill-color:#D9D9D9;--ltx-fg-color:#D9D9D9;" color="#D9D9D9" fill="#D9D9D9" stroke="#D9D9D9"><path d="M -157.48 56.69 L -157.48 78.74 L -141.73 78.74 C -110.24 78.74 -141.73 125.98 -94.49 125.98 C -47.24 125.98 -78.74 78.74 -47.24 78.74 L -31.5 78.74 L -31.5 56.69 L -157.48 56.69"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -157.48 56.69 L -157.48 78.74 L -141.73 78.74 C -110.24 78.74 -141.73 125.98 -94.49 125.98 C -47.24 125.98 -78.74 78.74 -47.24 78.74 L -31.5 78.74 L -31.5 56.69 L -157.48 56.69"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;" color="#FFFFFF" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF"><path d="M -94.49 94.49 M -78.74 94.49 C -78.74 103.19 -85.79 110.24 -94.49 110.24 C -103.19 110.24 -110.24 103.19 -110.24 94.49 C -110.24 85.79 -103.19 78.74 -94.49 78.74 C -85.79 78.74 -78.74 85.79 -78.74 94.49 Z M -94.49 94.49"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -94.49 94.49 M -78.74 94.49 C -78.74 103.19 -85.79 110.24 -94.49 110.24 C -103.19 110.24 -110.24 103.19 -110.24 94.49 C -110.24 85.79 -103.19 78.74 -94.49 78.74 C -85.79 78.74 -78.74 85.79 -78.74 94.49 Z M -94.49 94.49"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M -157.48 0 L -157.48 157.48 L -31.5 157.48 L -31.5 0 Z"></path><clipPath id="pgfcp15"><path d="M 157.48 69.29 M 236.22 69.29 C 236.22 112.78 200.97 148.03 157.48 148.03 C 113.99 148.03 78.74 112.78 78.74 69.29 C 78.74 25.8 113.99 -9.45 157.48 -9.45 C 200.97 -9.45 236.22 25.8 236.22 69.29 Z M 157.48 69.29"></path></clipPath><g clip-path="url(#pgfcp15)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 78.74 78.74 C 85.04 94.49 229.92 94.49 236.22 78.74"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#D9D9D9;--ltx-fill-color:#D9D9D9;--ltx-fg-color:#D9D9D9;" color="#D9D9D9" fill="#D9D9D9" stroke="#D9D9D9"><path d="M 81.89 -31.5 L 78.74 78.74 C 85.04 62.99 110.24 62.99 110.24 62.99 C 141.73 62.99 110.24 110.24 157.48 110.24 C 204.73 110.24 173.23 62.99 204.73 62.99 C 204.73 62.99 229.92 62.99 236.22 78.74 L 236.22 -31.5 L 81.89 -31.5"></path></g><g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 81.89 -31.5 L 78.74 78.74 C 85.04 62.99 110.24 62.99 110.24 62.99 C 141.73 62.99 110.24 110.24 157.48 110.24 C 204.73 110.24 173.23 62.99 204.73 62.99 C 204.73 62.99 229.92 62.99 236.22 78.74 L 236.22 -31.5 L 81.89 -31.5"></path></g><g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;" color="#FFFFFF" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF"><path d="M 157.48 78.74 M 173.23 78.74 C 173.23 87.44 166.18 94.49 157.48 94.49 C 148.78 94.49 141.73 87.44 141.73 78.74 C 141.73 70.04 148.78 62.99 157.48 62.99 C 166.18 62.99 173.23 70.04 173.23 78.74 Z M 157.48 78.74"></path></g><g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 157.48 78.74 M 173.23 78.74 C 173.23 87.44 166.18 94.49 157.48 94.49 C 148.78 94.49 141.73 87.44 141.73 78.74 C 141.73 70.04 148.78 62.99 157.48 62.99 C 166.18 62.99 173.23 70.04 173.23 78.74 Z M 157.48 78.74"></path></g><g></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 157.48 69.29 M 236.22 69.29 C 236.22 112.78 200.97 148.03 157.48 148.03 C 113.99 148.03 78.74 112.78 78.74 69.29 C 78.74 25.8 113.99 -9.45 157.48 -9.45 C 200.97 -9.45 236.22 25.8 236.22 69.29 Z M 157.48 69.29"></path></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>Tube in square and tube in a sphere, yielding the map <math id="S3.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}_{N,k}^{p}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}_{N,k}^{p}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The map <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}_{N,k}^{p}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}_{N,k}^{p}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a homotopy equivalence, however by a standard argument the connectivity tends to infinity as you stabilize <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N,k\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N,k\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> (in the model <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> the stabilization is given by direct sum with standard quadratic forms <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|^{2}-\|y\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\in\mathbf{R}^{a}\times\mathbf{R}^{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\in\mathbf{R}^{a}\times\mathbf{R}^{b}</annotation></semantics></math>). Indeed, given a tube bundle <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\subset M\times S^{N-1}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\subset M\times S^{N-1}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> over a compact manifold <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, so for each <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math> we have a tube <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{m}\subset M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{m}\subset M</annotation></semantics></math>. First, since the fiber of the fiber bundle <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is diffeomorphic to <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{k-1}\times S^{N-k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{k-1}\times S^{N-k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, by increasing <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> we may always find a section <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s:M\to\partial W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s:M\to\partial W</annotation></semantics></math> of the fibration <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial W\to M</annotation></semantics></math>. Second, as a section of the trivial bundle <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times S^{N-1}\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times S^{N-1}\to M</annotation></semantics></math> the section <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopic through sections to a constant section after increasing <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> if necessary. By isotopy extension this means we may assume that there is a point, say <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(0,\ldots,0,1)\in S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(0,\ldots,0,1)\in S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\partial T_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\partial T_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> for each tube <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{m}\subset S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{m}\subset S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, note that we may assume that <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{x}(\partial T_{m})=\mathbf{R}^{N-2}\times 0\times 0\subset\mathbf{R}^{N-1}\times 0=T_{x}S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{x}(\partial T_{m})=\mathbf{R}^{N-2}\times 0\times 0\subset\mathbf{R}^{N-1}\times 0=T_{x}S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in M</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, again by isotopy extension the only obstruction is linear algebraic and consists of trivializing the outwards pointing normal vector to <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial T_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial T_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, which lives in <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[M,S^{N-2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[M,S^{N-2}]</annotation></semantics></math> and hence dies if <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> becomes large enough. At this point it is straightforward to make <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> standard near an <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-ball about <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in B\subset S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in B\subset S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and then by a suitably isotopy of <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math> it is then straightforward to make it standard on the entire ball <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\subset S^{N-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\subset S^{N-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> has boundary this construction all works relative to <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial M</annotation></semantics></math> and so this proves that the inclusion <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}_{N,k}^{p}\subset\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}_{N,k}^{p}\subset\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}^{q}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes arbitrarily connected as <math id="S3.SS6.p8.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N,k\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N,k\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore we may speak unambiguously of the stable space of tubes <math id="S3.SS6.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the result of stabilizing <math id="S3.SS6.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N,k\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N,k\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, as this stable space is common to both of these models. The monoid <math id="S3.SS6.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{q}=\coprod_{N,k}\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∐</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>q</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{q}=\coprod_{N,k}\mathcal{T}_{N,k}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of functions of quadratic tube type which we consider in the present article was introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AACK</a>]</cite>, and the function space version of <math id="S3.SS6.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is realized by implementing stabilization of <math id="S3.SS6.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> as direct sum with suitable standard non-degenerate quadratic forms. The idea of thinking of tubes as codimension zero submanifolds of the sphere had already been introduced in the model of Rognes in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx56" title="" class="ltx_ref">R92</a>]</cite>. The advantage of this model is that it is better suited for the monoid (and indeed infinite loopspace) structures at play than the partition model used by Waldhausen. We also have a stable space <math id="S3.SS6.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> given by stabilizing <math id="S3.SS6.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N,k\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N,k\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS6.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{N,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{N,k}</annotation></semantics></math> in the same way, and the inclusion <math id="S3.SS6.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{k,N}\to\mathcal{T}_{k,N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{k,N}\to\mathcal{T}_{k,N}</annotation></semantics></math> stabilizes to a map <math id="S3.SS6.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, given a fibered function of tube type over <math id="S3.SS6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> we get an associated map <math id="S3.SS6.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>. If the fibered function is of rigid tube type then we get a lift <math id="S3.SS6.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to\mathcal{Q}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to\mathcal{Q}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> through <math id="S3.SS6.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, we may compose this with the equivalence <math id="S3.SS6.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\infty}\to BO" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\infty}\to BO</annotation></semantics></math> given by taking the negative eigenspace of a non-degenerate quadratic form, and the resulting map <math id="S3.SS6.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to BO" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to BO</annotation></semantics></math> classifies the stable bundle of the family of quadratic forms <math id="S3.SS6.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{m}:\mathbf{R}^{N}\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> comprising the fibered function of rigid tube type. Similarly, in the general case of a fibered function of tube type which is not necessarily rigid, one may consider the associated map <math id="S3.SS6.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> and it is shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wal82</a>]</cite> that the homotopy class of this map classifies the fibered function of tube type over <math id="S3.SS6.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> up to stable equivalence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The map <math id="S3.SS6.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> called the (stable) <span id="S3.SS6.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rigid tube map</span>, or more informally we may denote <math id="S3.SS6.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BO\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BO\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>. It was proved by Bökstedt that the stable rigid tube map is a rational homotopy equivalence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">B82</a>]</cite>, a fact which was also proved by the second author using higher Reidemeister torsion in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Igu02</a>]</cite>. For example, for homotopy groups this means that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{n}\mathcal{T}_{\infty}\otimes\mathbf{Q}\simeq\pi_{n}BO\otimes\mathbf{Q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{n}\mathcal{T}_{\infty}\otimes\mathbf{Q}\simeq\pi_{n}BO\otimes\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p11.2" class="ltx_p">which is <math id="S3.SS6.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.SS6.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is of the form <math id="S3.SS6.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4k</annotation></semantics></math> and is zero otherwise. For <math id="S3.SS6.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4k</annotation></semantics></math>, modulo the torsion subgroup, we have a monomorphism of free abelian groups</p>
<table id="S3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{4k}BO\xrightarrow{\sim}(\pi_{4k}\mathcal{T}_{\infty})/\ \text{Tors}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.722em">/</mo><mtext>Tors</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{4k}BO\xrightarrow{\sim}(\pi_{4k}\mathcal{T}_{\infty})/\ \text{Tors}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.p11.3" class="ltx_p">i.e. up to <math id="S3.SS6.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math> a monomorphism <math id="S3.SS6.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}\to\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}\to\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS6.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{k}\in\mathbf{Z}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{k}\in\mathbf{Z}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the index of the image. Higher torsion gives an injection <math id="S3.SS6.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}/J_{k}\mathbf{Z}\to\mathbf{R}/\lambda_{k}\mathbf{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐑</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐙</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}/J_{k}\mathbf{Z}\to\mathbf{R}/\lambda_{k}\mathbf{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS6.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}=p_{k}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}=p_{k}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS6.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to S^{4k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to S^{4k}</annotation></semantics></math> the generator ok <math id="S3.SS6.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="KO^{0}(S^{4k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">KO^{0}(S^{4k})</annotation></semantics></math>. So the higher torsion classes are a subtle enough invariant to detect the failure for the rigid tube map <math id="S3.SS6.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BO\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BO\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> to be surjective on homotopy groups modulo the torsion subgroup <math id="S3.SS6.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Tors}(\pi_{4k}\mathcal{T}_{\infty})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Tors</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Tors}(\pi_{4k}\mathcal{T}_{\infty})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wal82</a>]</cite> consider <math id="S3.SS6.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <span id="S3.SS6.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stable space of h-cobordisms of a point</span>, denoted <math id="S3.SS6.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a classifying space for fiber bundles <math id="S3.SS6.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\to M</annotation></semantics></math> whose fiber is an h-cobordism of a disk <math id="S3.SS6.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. diffeomorphic to <math id="S3.SS6.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{k}\times[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{k}\times[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> with a fixed trivialization of the lower boundary <math id="S3.SS6.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{k}\times\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{k}\times\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, up to stable equivalence. Waldhausen defined a map <math id="S3.SS6.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> given by stacking an h-cobordism of a disk on top of a standard tube, see Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3.6. The space of tubes ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.F5.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="299.77" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 403.03 299.77" width="403.03"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,299.77) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(158.04,0) translate(0,79.29)"><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -157.48 78.74 L -157.48 -78.74 L -39.37 -78.74 L -39.37 78.74 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fill-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fg-color:#BFBFBF;" color="#BFBFBF" fill="#BFBFBF" stroke="#BFBFBF" stroke-width="0.8pt"><path d="M -157.48 -78.74 L -157.48 0 C -106.3 0 -106.3 17.72 -118.11 17.72 C -125.98 19.69 -125.98 23.62 -118.11 25.59 C -102.36 27.56 -102.36 31.5 -125.98 33.46 C -129.92 37.4 -104.33 39.37 -108.27 41.34 C -110.24 53.15 -118.11 53.15 -94.49 57.09 C -78.74 61.02 -74.8 33.46 -86.61 21.65 C -98.43 9.84 -82.68 9.84 -94.49 1.97 C -110.24 -5.91 -110.24 -13.78 -78.74 -13.78 C -59.06 -9.84 -74.8 1.97 -62.99 9.84 C -51.18 9.84 -51.18 0 -39.37 0 L -39.37 -78.74 Z"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.4pt"><clipPath id="pgfcp16"><path d="M 39.37 157.48 M 102.36 157.48 C 102.36 192.27 74.16 220.47 39.37 220.47 C 4.58 220.47 -23.62 192.27 -23.62 157.48 C -23.62 122.69 4.58 94.49 39.37 94.49 C 74.16 94.49 102.36 122.69 102.36 157.48 Z M 39.37 157.48"></path></clipPath><g clip-path="url(#pgfcp16)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fill-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fg-color:#BFBFBF;" color="#BFBFBF" fill="#BFBFBF" stroke="#BFBFBF"><path d="M 39.37 141.73 M 100.39 141.73 C 100.39 152.6 73.07 161.42 39.37 161.42 C 5.67 161.42 -21.65 152.6 -21.65 141.73 C -21.65 130.86 5.67 122.05 39.37 122.05 C 73.07 122.05 100.39 130.86 100.39 141.73 Z M 39.37 141.73"></path></g><g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fill-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fg-color:#BFBFBF;" color="#BFBFBF" fill="#BFBFBF" stroke="#BFBFBF"><path d="M 39.37 149.61 M 106.3 149.61 C 106.3 160.48 76.33 169.29 39.37 169.29 C 2.41 169.29 -27.56 160.48 -27.56 149.61 C -27.56 138.73 2.41 129.92 39.37 129.92 C 76.33 129.92 106.3 138.73 106.3 149.61 Z M 39.37 149.61"></path></g><g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;" color="#FFFFFF" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF"><path d="M 39.37 157.48 M 102.36 157.48 C 102.36 168.35 74.16 177.17 39.37 177.17 C 4.58 177.17 -23.62 168.35 -23.62 157.48 C -23.62 146.61 4.58 137.8 39.37 137.8 C 74.16 137.8 102.36 146.61 102.36 157.48 Z M 39.37 157.48"></path></g><g></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 39.37 157.48 M 102.36 157.48 C 102.36 168.35 74.16 177.17 39.37 177.17 C 4.58 177.17 -23.62 168.35 -23.62 157.48 C -23.62 146.61 4.58 137.8 39.37 137.8 C 74.16 137.8 102.36 146.61 102.36 157.48 Z M 39.37 157.48"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 39.37 141.73 M 100.39 141.73 C 100.39 152.6 73.07 161.42 39.37 161.42 C 5.67 161.42 -21.65 152.6 -21.65 141.73 C -21.65 130.86 5.67 122.05 39.37 122.05 C 73.07 122.05 100.39 130.86 100.39 141.73 Z M 39.37 141.73"></path><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 39.37 157.48 M 102.36 157.48 C 102.36 192.27 74.16 220.47 39.37 220.47 C 4.58 220.47 -23.62 192.27 -23.62 157.48 C -23.62 122.69 4.58 94.49 39.37 94.49 C 74.16 94.49 102.36 122.69 102.36 157.48 Z M 39.37 157.48"></path></g><g></g></g><clipPath id="pgfcp17"><path d="M 151.5 2.68 M 232.12 2.68 C 232.12 47.21 196.03 83.31 151.5 83.31 C 106.97 83.31 70.87 47.21 70.87 2.68 C 70.87 -41.85 106.96 -77.95 151.49 -77.95 C 196.02 -77.95 232.12 -41.85 232.12 2.68 Z M 151.5 2.68"></path></clipPath><g clip-path="url(#pgfcp17)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fill-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fg-color:#BFBFBF;" color="#BFBFBF" fill="#BFBFBF" stroke="#BFBFBF"><path d="M 151.5 -17.48 M 229.6 -17.48 C 229.6 -3.56 194.63 7.72 151.5 7.72 C 108.36 7.72 73.39 -3.56 73.39 -17.48 C 73.39 -31.39 108.36 -42.67 151.49 -42.67 C 194.63 -42.67 229.6 -31.39 229.6 -17.48 Z M 151.5 -17.48"></path></g><g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fill-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fg-color:#BFBFBF;" color="#BFBFBF" fill="#BFBFBF" stroke="#BFBFBF"><path d="M 151.5 -7.4 M 237.16 -7.4 C 237.16 6.52 198.81 17.8 151.5 17.8 C 104.18 17.8 65.83 6.52 65.83 -7.4 C 65.83 -21.31 104.18 -32.59 151.5 -32.59 C 198.81 -32.59 237.16 -21.31 237.16 -7.4 Z M 151.5 -7.4"></path></g><g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;" color="#FFFFFF" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF"><path d="M 151.5 2.68 M 232.12 2.68 C 232.12 16.59 196.03 27.88 151.5 27.88 C 106.97 27.88 70.87 16.6 70.87 2.68 C 70.87 -11.23 106.97 -22.52 151.5 -22.52 C 196.02 -22.52 232.12 -11.24 232.12 2.68 Z M 151.5 2.68"></path></g><g></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 151.5 2.68 M 232.12 2.68 C 232.12 16.59 196.03 27.88 151.5 27.88 C 106.97 27.88 70.87 16.6 70.87 2.68 C 70.87 -11.23 106.97 -22.52 151.5 -22.52 C 196.02 -22.52 232.12 -11.24 232.12 2.68 Z M 151.5 2.68"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 151.5 -17.48 M 229.6 -17.48 C 229.6 -3.56 194.63 7.72 151.5 7.72 C 108.36 7.72 73.39 -3.56 73.39 -17.48 C 73.39 -31.39 108.36 -42.67 151.49 -42.67 C 194.63 -42.67 229.6 -31.39 229.6 -17.48 Z M 151.5 -17.48"></path><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 151.5 2.68 M 232.12 2.68 C 232.12 47.21 196.03 83.31 151.5 83.31 C 106.97 83.31 70.87 47.21 70.87 2.68 C 70.87 -41.85 106.96 -77.95 151.49 -77.95 C 196.02 -77.95 232.12 -41.85 232.12 2.68 Z M 151.5 2.68"></path></g><g></g></g><clipPath id="pgfcp18"><path d="M 187.47 -21.36 M 206.19 -18.73 C 204.73 -8.4 195.18 -1.2 184.84 -2.65 C 174.51 -4.1 167.31 -13.66 168.76 -23.99 C 170.21 -34.33 179.77 -41.53 190.1 -40.08 C 200.44 -38.62 207.64 -29.07 206.19 -18.73 Z M 187.47 -21.36"></path></clipPath><g clip-path="url(#pgfcp18)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;" color="#FFFFFF" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#FFFFFF"><path d="M 130.24 -21.45 C 170.78 -15.76 168.81 -1.72 159.45 -3.04 C 153 -2.35 152.56 0.77 158.58 3.2 C 170.83 6.51 170.39 9.63 151.46 8.56 C 147.9 11.24 167.96 15.65 164.62 16.77 C 161.75 25.91 155.51 25.03 173.78 30.78 C 185.82 35.65 192.01 14.26 183.97 3.59 C 175.92 -7.08 188.4 -5.33 179.92 -12.88 C 168.32 -20.87 169.2 -27.11 194.15 -23.6 C 209.3 -18.29 195.51 -10.69 203.99 -3.14 C 213.35 -1.82 214.45 -9.62 223.8 -8.31 C 193.27 -17.37 162.08 -21.75 130.24 -21.45"></path></g><g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fill-color:#BFBFBF;--ltx-fg-color:#BFBFBF;" color="#BFBFBF" fill="#BFBFBF" stroke="#BFBFBF" stroke-width="0.8pt"><path d="M 130.24 -21.45 C 170.78 -15.76 168.81 -1.72 159.45 -3.04 C 153 -2.35 152.56 0.77 158.58 3.2 C 170.83 6.51 170.39 9.63 151.46 8.56 C 147.9 11.24 167.96 15.65 164.62 16.77 C 161.75 25.91 155.51 25.03 173.78 30.78 C 185.82 35.65 192.01 14.26 183.97 3.59 C 175.92 -7.08 188.4 -5.33 179.92 -12.88 C 168.32 -20.87 169.2 -27.11 194.15 -23.6 C 209.3 -18.29 195.51 -10.69 203.99 -3.14 C 213.35 -1.82 214.45 -9.62 223.8 -8.31 C 195.24 -31.4 164.05 -35.79 130.24 -21.45"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 118.11 165.35"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 123 161.89)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:8.46em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="117"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F5.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">standard rigid tube</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 118.11 149.61"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 123 146.15)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.43em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="88.94"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.F5.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(q)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.F5.pic1.2" class="ltx_text"> of index <math id="S3.F5.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>On the left is an <math id="S3.F5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-cobordism of a disk representing an element of <math id="S3.F5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> as a partition of <math id="S3.F5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]^{n-1}\times[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]^{n-1}\times[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. On the right is the tube of index <math id="S3.F5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by pasting the <math id="S3.F5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>-cobordism into <math id="S3.F5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on top of the standard rigid tube of index <math id="S3.F5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> shown in the middle. This yields a map <math id="S3.F5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS6.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Consider also the forgetful map <math id="S3.SS6.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}^{\text{TOP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mtext>TOP</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}^{\text{TOP}}</annotation></semantics></math> in which <math id="S3.SS6.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\infty}^{\text{TOP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mtext>TOP</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\infty}^{\text{TOP}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the stable space of tubes in the topological category. It was shown by Waldhausen in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wal82</a>]</cite> that <math id="S3.SS6.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\infty}^{\text{TOP}}\simeq BG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mtext>TOP</mtext></msubsup><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\infty}^{\text{TOP}}\simeq BG</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS6.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BG</annotation></semantics></math> the classifying space for spherical fibrations, i.e. <math id="S3.SS6.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\lim_{N}G_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">lim</mo><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\lim_{N}G_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS6.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{N}</annotation></semantics></math> the space of maps <math id="S3.SS6.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{N}\to S^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{N}\to S^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S3.SS6.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem30" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem30.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.30</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem30.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wal82</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem30.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}\to BG" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}\to BG</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.Thmtheorem30.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a fibration sequence.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p14.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the map <math id="S3.SS6.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BG\to B(\mathbf{Z}/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BG\to B(\mathbf{Z}/2)</annotation></semantics></math> induced by the degree map <math id="S3.SS6.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\to\mathbf{Z}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\to\mathbf{Z}/2</annotation></semantics></math>. To a tube bundle <math id="S3.SS6.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to M</annotation></semantics></math>, with stable equivalence class classified by a map <math id="S3.SS6.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, is associated a map <math id="S3.SS6.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to B(\mathbf{Z}/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to B(\mathbf{Z}/2)</annotation></semantics></math>. This map is nullhomotopic precisely when <math id="S3.SS6.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is orientable. Higher torsion is not defined for non-orientable tube bundles, i.e. higher torsion is a characteristic class coming from the cohomology of the fiber of the map <math id="S3.SS6.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\infty}\to B(\mathbf{Z}/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\infty}\to B(\mathbf{Z}/2)</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the composition <math id="S3.SS6.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}\to BG\to B(\mathbf{Z}/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}\to BG\to B(\mathbf{Z}/2)</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial and so tube bundles which come from h-cobordism bundles are automatically orientable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p15.1" class="ltx_p">We also note that the higher torsion of a tube bundle changes sign under stabilization by the standard form <math id="S3.SS6.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{2}-y^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{2}-y^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS6.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐑</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> since the index <math id="S3.SS6.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> changes parity. For this reason it is better to assume that all ambient dimensions are even and always stabilize by a non-degenerate form of even index in an even dimensional ambient space, which always leaves torsion unchanged. With this modification of <math id="S3.SS6.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> (and similarly for <math id="S3.SS6.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>), any homotopy class of map <math id="S3.SS6.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the composite <math id="S3.SS6.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to B(\mathbf{Z}/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to B(\mathbf{Z}/2)</annotation></semantics></math> is null determines higher torsion classes in <math id="S3.SS6.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular any homotopy class of map <math id="S3.SS6.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to\mathcal{H}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to\mathcal{H}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> determines higher torsion classes in <math id="S3.SS6.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. The following observation, which will be our source of examples of Legendrians with non-trivial tube torsion in Section <a href="#S3.SS7" title="3.7. Legendrians with nontrivial tube torsion ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem31" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem31.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.31</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem31.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is a bundle of h-cobordisms of a disk classified by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to\mathcal{H}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to\mathcal{H}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\to M</annotation></semantics></math> is the stable tube bundle classified by the composition <math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\to\mathcal{H}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\to\mathcal{H}_{\infty}\to\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, then there holds <math id="S3.Thmtheorem31.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(V)=\tau_{k}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(V)=\tau_{k}(W)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS6.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS6.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">The tube bundle <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by gluing <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> to the trivial tube bundle <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times T</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> the standard tube. By Theorem <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem A.1. ‣ A.3. Framed functions ‣ Appendix A Moderate singularities ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> we may choose a function <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:V\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:V\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with moderate singularities which is standard near the top and bottom of each h-cobordism, i.e. such that on each fiber <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{m}\times[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{m}\times[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> the restriction of <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is standard near top and bottom. We may glue such a function <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to the standard quadratic form <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q:M\times T\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q:M\times T\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> on the trivial tube bundle <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times T</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain a function with moderate singularities <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:W\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:W\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> whose paramerized Morse theory consists of the union of that of <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and (the constant family) <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof is then completed by applying the framing principle Theorem <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem B.3. ‣ B.5. The framing principle ‣ Appendix B Higher torsion of fiber bundles ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a> to compute <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> using the function <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:W\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:W\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> on the glued up tube bundle and to compute <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{k}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{k}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> using the function <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:V\to\mathbf{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐑</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:V\to\mathbf{R}</annotation></semantics></math> on the original bundle of h-cobordisms. The two computations yields the same expression because the extra contribution from <math id="S3.SS6.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> to both the parametrized Morse complex and to the stable bundle is trivial. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem32" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem32.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.32</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem32.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem32.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem32.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a Legendrian <math id="S3.Thmtheorem32.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda\subset J^{1}M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda\subset J^{1}M</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the subset <math id="S3.Thmtheorem32.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(\Lambda)\subset[M,\mathcal{T}_{\infty}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(\Lambda)\subset[M,\mathcal{T}_{\infty}]</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of homotopy classes of maps classifying a stable tube bundle on which <math id="S3.Thmtheorem32.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda</annotation></semantics></math> admits a generating function. The homotopy lifting theorem for generating functions implies that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem32.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> is a Legendrian isotopy invariant. The tube torsion classes that we consider in the present article are a cohomological shadow of the a priori finer invariant <math id="S3.Thmtheorem32.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(\Lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(\Lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem33" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem33.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.33</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem33.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem33.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem33.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By the stable parametrized stable h-cobordism theorem of Waldhausen <math id="S3.Thmtheorem33.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is closely related to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem33.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\mathbf{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐒</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\mathbf{S})</annotation></semantics></math>, the algebraic K-theory of the sphere spectrum, i.e. <math id="S3.Thmtheorem33.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\ast)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\ast)</annotation></semantics></math>, the Waldhausen K-theory of a point. Hence by Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem30" title="Theorem 3.30 ([]). ‣ 3.6. The space of tubes ‣ 3. Legendrian tube torsion ‣ Legendrian and Lagrangian higher torsion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.30</span></a> the stable space of tubes <math id="S3.Thmtheorem33.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> is also closely related to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem33.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\ast)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\ast)</annotation></semantics></math>. As noted in the remark after Proposition 3.1 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wal82</a>]</cite>, there is a homotopy cartesian square</p>
<table id="S3.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
